export const UNIT4_SEGMENT_B_ITEMS = [
  {"id": "4.6-basic-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.5", "prompt": "A student wants to write a Java program that reads data from a text file using the File and Scanner classes. Which import statement is required for the File class and the IOException class used when handling file-reading errors?", "choices": ["import java.io.*;", "import java.util.*;", "import java.text.*;", "import java.file.*;"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "File and IOException are part of the java.io package, so import java.io.*; (or importing File and IOException individually from java.io) is required (4.6.A.5). java.util is where Scanner itself lives, not File or IOException, java.text is not part of this course's file-handling classes, and java.file is not a real Java package."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.3", "prompt": "Which of the following correctly creates a File object that refers to a text file named \"roster.txt\" located in the program's working directory?", "choices": ["File roster = new File();", "File roster = new File(\"roster.txt\");", "File roster = new Scanner(\"roster.txt\");", "File roster = \"roster.txt\";"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The File class provides a constructor File(String str) where str is the pathname of the file, so new File(\"roster.txt\") correctly creates a File object referring to that file (4.6.A.3). Option A omits the required pathname argument, option C incorrectly tries to assign a Scanner object to a File variable, and option D attempts to assign a String directly to a File variable without calling a constructor, neither of which compiles."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.4", "prompt": "A method attempts to open a text file using the File and Scanner classes, as shown below.\npublic void loadData()\n{\n    File dataFile = new File(\"inventory.txt\");\n    Scanner input = new Scanner(dataFile);\n}\nThis method does not compile. Which change would fix the compilation error?", "choices": ["Changing File dataFile to Scanner dataFile", "Removing the argument from the Scanner constructor", "Changing the method header to public void loadData() throws IOException", "Changing the method header to private void loadData()"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Creating a Scanner from a File can fail if the file cannot be opened, so Java requires the possible IOException to be handled, most simply by declaring throws IOException in the method header (4.6.A.4). Swapping the declared types of dataFile and input would break the code entirely, removing the constructor's argument would prevent the Scanner from connecting to any file, and changing only the method's visibility does not address the unhandled exception."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "Which of the following correctly constructs a Scanner object named input that reads from a File object named dataFile?", "choices": ["Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);", "Scanner input = new File(dataFile);", "Scanner input = dataFile.next();", "Scanner input = new Scanner(dataFile);"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The Scanner class provides a constructor Scanner(File f) that connects the new Scanner object to the given File object, so new Scanner(dataFile) is correct (4.6.A.6). Constructing a Scanner from System.in reads from the keyboard rather than a file, which is out of scope for file reading, assigning a File object to a Scanner variable does not compile, and calling next() before a Scanner exists makes no sense."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "The text file scores.txt contains the following text.\n10 20 30\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from scores.txt.\nFile scoreFile = new File(\"scores.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(scoreFile);\nint a = input.nextInt();\nint b = input.nextInt();\nint c = input.nextInt();\nWhat are the values of a, b, and c after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["a is 10, b is 20, and c is 30", "a is 30, b is 20, and c is 10", "a, b, and c are all equal to 10", "a is 10, b is 10, and c is 10"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Each call to nextInt() reads the next whitespace-separated int token from the file in order, so the three calls read 10, then 20, then 30 into a, b, and c respectively (4.6.A.6). The tokens are not read in reverse order, and nothing causes the same value to be read repeatedly."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "The text file temps.txt contains the single line of text shown below.\nsunny\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from temps.txt.\nFile weatherFile = new File(\"temps.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(weatherFile);\ndouble reading = input.nextDouble();\nWhat happens when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["reading is assigned the value 0.0", "An InputMismatchException is thrown, since the next token in the file is not a valid double", "reading is assigned the String \"sunny\"", "The file is automatically skipped and the program continues normally"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The nextDouble() method throws an InputMismatchException if the next token in the file cannot be interpreted as a double, and \"sunny\" is not a valid double, so the exception is thrown (4.6.A.6). nextDouble() does not silently default to 0.0, it cannot return a String value since its return type is double, and Java does not automatically skip over invalid tokens on its own."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "The text file greeting.txt contains the following line of text.\nWelcome to the store\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from greeting.txt.\nFile msgFile = new File(\"greeting.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(msgFile);\nString message = input.nextLine();\nWhat is the value of message after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["\"Welcome\"", "\"W\"", "\"Welcome to the store\"", "An empty String"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The nextLine() method returns the entire next line of text from the file as a single String, so message is assigned the full line \"Welcome to the store\" (4.6.A.6). nextLine() does not stop at the first word or the first character, and since the file actually contains text, message is not left empty."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "The text file colors.txt contains the following text.\nred green blue\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from colors.txt.\nFile colorFile = new File(\"colors.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(colorFile);\nString first = input.next();\nWhat is the value of first after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["\"red green blue\"", "\"blue\"", "\"green\"", "\"red\""], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The next() method returns the next whitespace-separated String token from the file, and the first token in the file is \"red\", so first is assigned \"red\" (4.6.A.6). next() does not return the entire line at once, and it does not skip ahead to later tokens like \"green\" or \"blue\"."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "Which of the following best describes the value returned by a call to hasNext() on a Scanner object connected to a file?", "choices": ["true if there is at least one more token left to be read from the file, and false otherwise", "The total number of tokens remaining in the file", "The next String token in the file, without removing it", "true only if the file has already been completely read"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "hasNext() returns a boolean value: true if the Scanner has at least one more token available to read, and false once there is nothing left, which is why it is used as the condition of a loop that reads an entire file (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.9). It does not return a count of remaining tokens, it does not return the token itself, and it returns true rather than false whenever more input remains."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.10", "prompt": "A program finishes reading all the data it needs from a text file using a Scanner object named input. Which of the following should be done immediately after the program is finished using input?", "choices": ["input.hasNext();", "input.close();", "input.nextLine();", "input = new File(\"data.txt\");"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A file should be closed with close() once a program is finished reading from it, which releases the system resources associated with the open file (4.6.A.10). Calling hasNext() or nextLine() again does not close the file, and reassigning input to a File object mismatches the variable's declared Scanner type and does not close anything."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.1", "prompt": "A student writes a program that stores a list of high scores in a variable while the program is running, but the scores disappear the next time the program is started. Which of the following best explains why using a text file would solve this problem?", "choices": ["A text file automatically runs faster than a variable stored in memory", "A text file can only be read by the program that created it", "A file is storage for data that persists even when the program is not running, so scores saved to a file remain available the next time the program starts", "A text file cannot be opened by more than one program at a time"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A file provides storage for data that persists beyond a single run of a program, so writing the high scores to a text file would let that data survive after the program ends and be available again the next time it runs (4.6.A.1). Speed of access is not the relevant issue here, files are not restricted to being read only by the program that created them, and whether multiple programs can open a file at once is unrelated to the persistence problem described."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "The text file teams.txt contains the following text.\nHawks Eagles Wolves\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from teams.txt.\nFile teamFile = new File(\"teams.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(teamFile);\nString t1 = input.next();\nString t2 = input.next();\nWhat are the values of t1 and t2 after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["t1 is \"Hawks Eagles Wolves\" and t2 is an empty String", "t1 is \"Wolves\" and t2 is \"Eagles\"", "t1 and t2 are both \"Hawks\"", "t1 is \"Hawks\" and t2 is \"Eagles\""], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Each call to next() reads the next whitespace-separated String token in order, so the first call returns \"Hawks\" and the second call returns \"Eagles\" (4.6.A.6). The tokens are not read in reverse order, next() does not return the entire file's text at once, and successive calls to next() do not return the same token twice."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.8", "prompt": "A program reads the String line, which has the value \"apple,banana,cherry\", from a text file. Which of the following statements correctly splits line into an array of three Strings using the comma as a delimiter?", "choices": ["String[] items = line.split(\",\");", "String[] items = line.split(\".\");", "String[] items = line.substring(\",\");", "String[] items = line.indexOf(\",\");"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The split(String del) method divides a String into an array of Strings using the given delimiter, so line.split(\",\") correctly separates the three comma-delimited values into an array (4.6.A.8). Using \".\" as the delimiter would look for periods rather than commas, substring does not accept a delimiter argument in this way, and indexOf returns a single int position rather than a String array."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "The text file flags.txt contains the following text.\ntrue false true\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from flags.txt.\nFile flagFile = new File(\"flags.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(flagFile);\nboolean first = input.nextBoolean();\nWhat is the value of first after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["false", "true", "\"true\"", "1"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The nextBoolean() method reads the next token from the file and returns it as a boolean value, and since the first token in the file is \"true\", first is assigned the boolean value true (4.6.A.6). The value is not false, since \"false\" is not the first token read, and nextBoolean() returns a primitive boolean rather than a String or an int."},
  {"id": "4.6-basic-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "A programmer wants to read every token from a text file without knowing in advance how many tokens the file contains. Which of the following loop headers represents the standard pattern for this task, assuming input is a valid Scanner object connected to the file?", "choices": ["for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)", "if (input.hasNext())", "while (input.hasNext())", "while (input.nextInt() > 0)"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A while loop with hasNext() as its condition is the standard pattern for reading an entire file, since it keeps looping for as long as another token remains and stops naturally once the file is exhausted (4.6.A.9). A for loop with a fixed bound of 10 assumes a known token count that may not match the file, an if statement only checks once instead of repeating, and looping based on nextInt() > 0 both assumes the tokens are all ints and consumes a token just to test the condition."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file reviews.txt contains an unknown number of integer ratings, one or more per line. In the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from reviews.txt.\nFile reviewFile = new File(\"reviews.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(reviewFile);\nint count = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    int rating = input.nextInt();\n    count++;\n}\nSystem.out.println(count);\ninput.close();\nWhat does the value printed by this code segment represent?", "choices": ["The value of the very last rating in the file", "The sum of all the ratings in the file", "The number of lines contained in the file", "The total number of integer ratings contained in the file"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Each iteration of the loop reads one more int token and increments count by one, so after the loop finishes, count holds the total number of int tokens (ratings) that were read from the file (4.6.A.9). The variable rating itself, not count, would hold the last value read, count never adds the ratings together to form a sum, and hasNext() checks for tokens rather than lines, so count does not correspond to the number of lines."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "The text file donations.txt contains only numerical data and spaces, representing donation amounts. In the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from donations.txt.\nFile donationFile = new File(\"donations.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(donationFile);\ndouble total = 0.0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    if (input.nextDouble() >= 20.0)\n    {\n        total = total + input.nextDouble();\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(total);\nThis code segment is intended to print the sum of all donation amounts that are at least 20.0. Which best describes the actual behavior of the code segment?", "choices": ["It calls nextDouble() twice per qualifying iteration, so it may add in the wrong value or skip values, producing an incorrect sum", "It correctly prints the sum of all donation amounts that are at least 20.0", "It prints the sum of every donation amount in the file, regardless of size", "It throws an InputMismatchException every time it runs"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Because nextDouble() is called once in the if condition to test the value and again inside the block to add it, each qualifying donation actually consumes two separate tokens from the file, so the value that gets added is not necessarily the same one that was tested, and values can be skipped entirely (4.6.A.6). The code does not correctly compute the intended sum, it does not add every donation regardless of size since the condition still filters some out, and no exception is thrown as long as the file contains only valid numeric tokens."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file readings.txt contains the following text, with no other characters.\n15 42 8 91\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named source is created to read from readings.txt.\nint latest = 0;\nFile sensorFile = new File(\"readings.txt\");\nScanner source = new Scanner(sensorFile);\nwhile (source.hasNext())\n{\n    latest = source.nextInt();\n}\nSystem.out.println(latest);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["0", "91", "15", "156"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Each pass through the loop reassigns latest to the next int read from the file, with no accumulation taking place, so by the time hasNext() becomes false, latest holds only the last value read from the file, which is 91 (4.6.A.9). latest does not remain at its initial value of 0, it is not left holding the first value read, and the code never combines the digits or values into 156."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "The text file wages.txt contains only numerical data and spaces, representing hourly wages. A programmer wants the following code segment to correctly print the sum of all wages that are greater than 15.0.\nFile wageFile = new File(\"wages.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(wageFile);\ndouble total = 0.0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    /* missing code */\n}\nSystem.out.println(total);\nWhich of the following can replace /* missing code */ so the code segment works as intended?", "choices": ["if (input.nextDouble() > 15.0)\n{\n    total = total + input.nextDouble();\n}", "total = total + input.nextDouble();", "double wage = input.nextDouble();\nif (wage > 15.0)\n{\n    total = total + wage;\n}", "if (total > 15.0)\n{\n    total = total + input.nextDouble();\n}"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Reading the value once into the variable wage, then testing and adding that same stored value, ensures each token is consumed exactly once per iteration and that the value tested is the same value added (4.6.A.6). The first choice calls nextDouble() twice per qualifying iteration, consuming two tokens and risking an incorrect sum; the second choice adds every wage without ever checking whether it exceeds 15.0; and the fourth choice compares the accumulator total rather than the newly read value, which does not test each wage correctly."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file animals.txt contains the following text.\ncat dog cow bat rat\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from animals.txt.\nFile animalFile = new File(\"animals.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(animalFile);\nint count = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    String word = input.next();\n    if (word.indexOf(\"at\") >= 0)\n    {\n        count++;\n    }\n}\ninput.close();\nSystem.out.println(count);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["5", "2", "0", "3"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The words \"cat\", \"bat\", and \"rat\" each contain the substring \"at\" at some position, so indexOf(\"at\") returns a value greater than or equal to 0 for exactly those three words, making count equal to 3 (4.6.A.9). \"dog\" and \"cow\" do not contain \"at\" anywhere, so count is not 5, and count is not 2 or 0 since exactly three, not two or zero, of the five words contain \"at\"."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.8", "prompt": "The text file pairs.txt contains the following single line of text.\nred blue green\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from pairs.txt.\nFile pairFile = new File(\"pairs.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(pairFile);\nString line = input.nextLine();\nString[] words = line.split(\" \");\nSystem.out.println(words.length);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["3", "1", "0", "6"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The nextLine() call reads the entire line \"red blue green\" as a single String, and split(\" \") divides that String around each space, producing an array of the three separate words, so words.length is 3 (4.6.A.8). The line is not treated as a single unsplit token, split does not return an empty array when a valid delimiter is found, and there are only 3 words, not 6, in the line."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file balances.txt contains only integer values and spaces, some of which may be negative. A programmer wants to count how many values in the file are negative. Three implementations are shown below, each assuming input is a valid Scanner object connected to balances.txt.\nImplementation I\nint count = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    int val = input.nextInt();\n    if (val < 0)\n    {\n        count++;\n    }\n}\nImplementation II\nint count = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    if (input.nextInt() < 0)\n    {\n        count++;\n    }\n}\nImplementation III\nint count = 0;\nint val = input.nextInt();\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    if (val < 0)\n    {\n        count++;\n    }\n}\nWhich of these implementations correctly counts the number of negative values in the file?", "choices": ["I only", "I and II only", "II only", "I, II, and III"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Implementation I reads each value into val once per iteration and correctly tests it, and Implementation II reads and tests the same value in a single nextInt() call without reading it a second time, so both correctly count negative values (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.9). Implementation III reads only one value before the loop begins and never reads another value inside the loop body, so it repeatedly tests the same unchanging val, making it incorrect, and its condition also never consumes new tokens, risking an infinite loop."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "The text file distances.txt contains the following text.\n5.5 3.2 8.1\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from distances.txt.\nFile distFile = new File(\"distances.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(distFile);\nint first = input.nextInt();\nWhat happens when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["first is assigned the value 5", "first is assigned the value 5.5", "An InputMismatchException is thrown, since \"5.5\" is not a valid int token", "first is assigned the value 0"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The token \"5.5\" cannot be interpreted as an int because it contains a decimal point, so calling nextInt() on it throws an InputMismatchException rather than truncating or rounding the value (4.6.A.6). first is never assigned 5, 5.5, or 0, since the exception is thrown before any assignment to first can complete."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file entries.txt contains the following text.\n4 8 15 16 23\nA programmer wants to read every value from entries.txt into an int array, without knowing in advance exactly how many values the file contains, but knowing the file contains at most 10 values. In the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from entries.txt.\nFile entryFile = new File(\"entries.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(entryFile);\nint[] values = new int[10];\nint count = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    values[count] = input.nextInt();\n    count++;\n}\nAfter this code segment executes, which best describes the contents of values and the value of count?", "choices": ["values contains 4, 8, 15, 16, and 23 in indexes 0 through 4, and count is 10", "values contains ten copies of 23, and count is 10", "values remains entirely filled with 0s, and count is 0", "values contains 4, 8, 15, 16, and 23 in indexes 0 through 4, and count is 5"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The loop reads one token per iteration into values[count] and then increments count, so after all five tokens in the file are read, indexes 0 through 4 hold 4, 8, 15, 16, and 23 in order, and count correctly equals 5, matching the number of tokens actually read (4.6.A.9). count does not reach 10, since hasNext() becomes false once the file's five tokens are exhausted, values is not filled with repeated copies of the last value, and since the file does contain data, values and count are not left at their default states."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file quizzes.txt contains only integer quiz scores separated by spaces. A programmer wants to compute the average score. Two implementations are shown below, each assuming input is a valid Scanner object connected to quizzes.txt.\nImplementation I\nint total = 0;\nint count = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    total = total + input.nextInt();\n    count++;\n}\ndouble average = (double) total / count;\nImplementation II\nint total = 0;\nint count = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    total = total + input.nextInt();\n    count = count + input.nextInt();\n}\ndouble average = (double) total / count;\nWhich implementation correctly computes the average of the scores in the file?", "choices": ["Implementation I only", "Implementation II only", "Both implementations compute the correct average", "Neither implementation compiles"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Implementation I reads one score per iteration into total and separately increments count by one to track how many scores were read, correctly computing the average (4.6.A.9). Implementation II calls nextInt() a second time inside the loop to update count, consuming an extra token from the file each iteration, so count no longer represents the number of scores read and the resulting average is incorrect; both implementations do compile, since the syntax itself is valid."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file words.txt contains the following text.\nowl fox bear elephant ox\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from words.txt.\nFile wordFile = new File(\"words.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(wordFile);\nString longest = \"\";\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    String word = input.next();\n    if (word.length() > longest.length())\n    {\n        longest = word;\n    }\n}\ninput.close();\nSystem.out.println(longest);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["\"owl\"", "\"elephant\"", "\"bear\"", "\"ox\""], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The loop compares the length of each word read against the length of the longest word found so far, updating longest whenever a longer word is found, and \"elephant\" has more characters than any other word in the file, so it ends up stored in longest (4.6.A.9). \"owl\", \"bear\", and \"ox\" are each shorter than \"elephant\" and get replaced as longer words are encountered during the traversal."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.10", "prompt": "A method reads all the data it needs from a text file using a Scanner object named fileReader, but the method never calls fileReader.close(). Which of the following best describes the consequence of omitting this call?", "choices": ["The program will not compile", "The values already read from the file using fileReader become invalid", "The file remains open longer than necessary, which can waste system resources even though the program may still run correctly", "The program will throw an InputMismatchException the next time it runs"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A Scanner connected to a file should be closed with close() once the program is finished using it; omitting this call does not stop the program from compiling or running, but it leaves the underlying file resource open longer than needed, which can waste system resources (4.6.A.10). The values already successfully read remain valid regardless of whether close() is later called, and forgetting to close a file does not by itself cause an InputMismatchException."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file bids.txt contains only positive integer bid amounts separated by spaces. A programmer wants to determine the highest bid in the file. Which of the following code segments correctly stores the highest bid in the variable maxBid, assuming input is a valid Scanner object connected to bids.txt and the file contains at least one value?", "choices": ["int maxBid = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    if (input.nextInt() > maxBid)\n    {\n        maxBid = input.nextInt();\n    }\n}", "int maxBid = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    maxBid = input.nextInt();\n}", "int maxBid = input.nextInt();\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    maxBid = input.nextInt();\n}", "int maxBid = input.nextInt();\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    int bid = input.nextInt();\n    if (bid > maxBid)\n    {\n        maxBid = bid;\n    }\n}"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Reading the first bid before the loop to initialize maxBid, then reading and comparing each remaining bid exactly once per iteration, correctly identifies the largest value in the file (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.9). The first choice calls nextInt() twice per qualifying iteration, consuming an extra token and comparing against a different value than the one stored; the second and third choices simply overwrite maxBid with each value read and never compare against the running maximum, so they end up holding only the last value in the file."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file schedule.txt contains three lines of text, and each line contains exactly two words separated by a single space. In the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from schedule.txt.\nFile schedFile = new File(\"schedule.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(schedFile);\nint tokenCount = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    String piece = input.next();\n    tokenCount++;\n}\ninput.close();\nSystem.out.println(tokenCount);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["6", "3", "2", "0"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Since next() reads individual whitespace-separated tokens rather than whole lines, and the file contains three lines of two words each, there are 6 total tokens for the loop to read, so tokenCount ends at 6 (4.6.A.9). tokenCount does not equal 3, the number of lines, since hasNext() and next() operate on tokens rather than lines, and it does not equal 2 or 0 either."},
  {"id": "4.6-intermediate-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.6.A.8", "prompt": "The text file record.txt contains the following single line of text.\nSanchez,102,Freshman\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from record.txt.\nFile recFile = new File(\"record.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(recFile);\nString line = input.nextLine();\nString[] fields = line.split(\",\");\nString name = fields[0];\nString grade = fields[2];\nSystem.out.println(name + \" \" + grade);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["\"Sanchez 102\"", "\"Sanchez Freshman\"", "\"102 Freshman\"", "\"Sanchez,102,Freshman\""], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Splitting the line around each comma produces the array {\"Sanchez\", \"102\", \"Freshman\"}, so fields[0] is \"Sanchez\" and fields[2] is \"Freshman\", making the printed result \"Sanchez Freshman\" (4.6.A.8). fields[0] is not paired with fields[1] as the first choice suggests, fields[2] is not paired with fields[1] as the third choice suggests, and the commas are removed by split rather than left in the output as the fourth choice suggests."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file examResults.txt contains an unknown number of integer exam scores, separated by spaces. A programmer wants to compute passCount, the number of scores that are at least 70, and passSum, the sum of only those passing scores. Four implementations are shown below, each assuming input is a valid Scanner object connected to examResults.txt.\nImplementation I\nint passCount = 0;\nint passSum = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    int score = input.nextInt();\n    if (score >= 70)\n    {\n        passCount++;\n        passSum = passSum + score;\n    }\n}\nImplementation II\nint passCount = 0;\nint passSum = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    if (input.nextInt() >= 70)\n    {\n        passCount++;\n        passSum = passSum + input.nextInt();\n    }\n}\nImplementation III\nint passCount = 0;\nint passSum = 0;\nint score = input.nextInt();\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    if (score >= 70)\n    {\n        passCount++;\n        passSum = passSum + score;\n    }\n}\nImplementation IV\nint passCount = 0;\nint passSum = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    int score = input.nextInt();\n    if (score >= 70)\n    {\n        passSum = passSum + score;\n    }\n    passCount++;\n}\nWhich implementation, if any, correctly computes both passCount and passSum?", "choices": ["Implementation III only", "Implementation IV only", "Implementation I only", "Implementation II only"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Implementation I reads each score exactly once per iteration into score, then increments passCount and adds to passSum only when that score qualifies, correctly tracking both values (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.9). Implementation II calls nextInt() a second time inside the if block, consuming an extra token so the value added to passSum is not the same value that was tested; Implementation III reads only a single score before the loop and never updates it inside the loop, so it repeatedly tests and adds the same unchanging value; Implementation IV increments passCount for every score read, including scores below 70, so passCount no longer reflects only the passing scores."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.4", "prompt": "A programmer writes the following method to read integer inventory counts from a text file and return their sum.\npublic int totalInventory(String filename)\n{\n    File invFile = new File(filename);\n    Scanner input = new Scanner(invFile);\n    int sum = 0;\n    while (input.hasNext())\n    {\n        sum = sum + input.nextInt();\n    }\n    return sum;\n}\nThis method does not compile. Consider the following statements about why it fails and how to fix it.\nI. The method fails to compile because creating a Scanner from a File can throw a checked IOException that is never handled.\nII. Adding throws IOException to the method header would allow the method to compile.\nIII. The method also forgets to call input.close(), which by itself would cause a separate compilation error.\nIV. Wrapping the Scanner creation in a try-catch block that catches IOException would also allow the method to compile.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I and III only", "I, II, and III only", "II and IV only", "I, II, and IV only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Statements I, II, and IV are true: the Scanner(File) constructor can throw a checked IOException that must be handled, and this can be resolved either by declaring throws IOException in the method header or by catching the exception directly in a try-catch block (4.6.A.4). Statement III is false because forgetting to call close() does not cause a compilation error at all; it is a resource-management oversight, not a syntax or checked-exception problem, so it does not, by itself, prevent the code from compiling."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file shipments.txt contains an unknown number of integer package weights, in pounds, separated by spaces. A warehouse wants to count how many packages are \"light\" (under 10), how many are \"standard\" (10 to 49, inclusive), and how many are \"heavy\" (50 or more). Consider the following code segment.\nFile shipFile = new File(\"shipments.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(shipFile);\nint light = 0;\nint standard = 0;\nint heavy = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    int weight = input.nextInt();\n    if (weight < 10)\n    {\n        light++;\n    }\n    else if (weight < 50)\n    {\n        standard++;\n    }\n    else\n    {\n        heavy++;\n    }\n}\ninput.close();\nIf shipments.txt contains the values 5 12 60 8 49 50 3, what are the final values of light, standard, and heavy?", "choices": ["light is 3, standard is 2, heavy is 2", "light is 2, standard is 3, heavy is 2", "light is 3, standard is 3, heavy is 1", "light is 2, standard is 2, heavy is 3"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Tracing each value in order \u2014 5, 8, and 3 are each less than 10 and increment light; 12 and 49 fall in the 10-to-49 range and increment standard; and 60 and 50 are each 50 or more and increment heavy \u2014 yields light equal to 3, standard equal to 2, and heavy equal to 2 (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.9). The other choices miscount which values fall into each category, most commonly by misclassifying the boundary values 49 or 50."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.6", "prompt": "The text file mixedData.txt contains the following text.\n12 8 hello 4\nA programmer writes the following code segment, intending to read and sum every integer in the file.\nFile dataFile = new File(\"mixedData.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(dataFile);\nint sum = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    sum = sum + input.nextInt();\n}\nSystem.out.println(sum);\nWhat happens when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["The code segment prints 24, correctly summing 12, 8, and 4", "An InputMismatchException is thrown once the Scanner attempts to read \"hello\" as an int", "The code segment prints 20, skipping over \"hello\" automatically", "The code segment prints 0, since the file contains a non-numeric token"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The loop calls nextInt() on every token as long as hasNext() is true, and hasNext() only checks whether another token exists, not whether it is numeric, so when the Scanner reaches the token \"hello\" and nextInt() attempts to read it as an int, an InputMismatchException is thrown (4.6.A.6). The code does not print 24 or 20, since it never reaches the final value 4 without crashing first, Scanner does not automatically skip non-numeric tokens on its own, and the program does not simply print 0 \u2014 it throws an exception and stops."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "A programmer writes the following code segment to compute the average of all the double values in the text file measurements.txt, which contains only numerical data and spaces.\nFile measureFile = new File(\"measurements.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(measureFile);\ndouble total = 0.0;\nint count = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    total = total + input.nextDouble();\n    count++;\n}\ndouble average = total / count;\nConsider the following statements about this code segment.\nI. The code segment correctly computes the average of every value in the file.\nII. The loop consumes exactly one token from the file per iteration.\nIII. If measurements.txt is empty, this code segment fails to compile, since count is never incremented in that case.\nIV. Replacing hasNext() in the while condition with hasNextDouble() would still allow the loop to correctly process a file containing only valid double values.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I and III only", "II and IV only", "I, II, and IV only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Statements I, II, and IV are true: the loop reads exactly one double per iteration and accumulates it correctly into total while incrementing count, so average is computed correctly; and since hasNextDouble() would also return true whenever another double-parsable token remains, substituting it in place of hasNext() would still work correctly for a file containing only valid double values (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.9). Statement III is false because whether measurements.txt happens to be empty is a runtime detail with no bearing on whether the code compiles; count would simply remain 0 in that case, which is not a compilation error at all, just a condition that would make total / count evaluate strangely at runtime."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file inventoryTags.txt contains the following text.\nSKU100 SKU245 REF100 SKU330 REF221 SKU410\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from inventoryTags.txt.\nFile tagFile = new File(\"inventoryTags.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(tagFile);\nint skuCount = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    String tag = input.next();\n    if (tag.indexOf(\"SKU\") == 0)\n    {\n        skuCount++;\n    }\n}\ninput.close();\nSystem.out.println(skuCount);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["2", "3", "5", "4"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The condition tag.indexOf(\"SKU\") == 0 is true only when the token contains \"SKU\" starting at position 0 (that is, the token begins with \"SKU\"), which is the case for \"SKU100\", \"SKU245\", \"SKU330\", and \"SKU410\", giving a total of 4 (4.6.A.9). The tokens \"REF100\" and \"REF221\" do not contain \"SKU\" anywhere, so indexOf returns -1 for them rather than 0, and the other choices either undercount or overcount the qualifying tags."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file dailyTemps.txt contains an unknown number of integer temperature readings, separated by spaces, recorded once per hour over several days, with no markers separating days. A programmer wants to find the longest streak of consecutive readings that are all at least 32. Consider the following code segment.\nFile tempFile = new File(\"dailyTemps.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(tempFile);\nint currentStreak = 0;\nint longestStreak = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    int reading = input.nextInt();\n    if (reading >= 32)\n    {\n        currentStreak++;\n        if (currentStreak > longestStreak)\n        {\n            longestStreak = currentStreak;\n        }\n    }\n    else\n    {\n        currentStreak = 0;\n    }\n}\ninput.close();\nSystem.out.println(longestStreak);\nIf dailyTemps.txt contains the values 30 33 35 40 28 31 36 38 41 20, what is printed?", "choices": ["3", "4", "2", "10"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Tracing the readings in order, the streak of values at least 32 reaches a maximum length of 3 (33, 35, 40), since the value 28 breaks the streak before it can grow further, and the later streak of 36, 38, 41 also reaches length 3 but does not exceed the previously recorded longestStreak (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.9). The streak never actually reaches 4 readings in a row, it is not limited to just 2, and 10 is simply the total number of readings in the file, not the length of the longest qualifying streak."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.5", "prompt": "A programmer writes the following class to read integer temperatures from a file and compute their sum.\npublic class TempReader\n{\n    public int sumTemps(String filename) throws IOException\n    {\n        File tempFile = new File(filename);\n        Scanner input = new Scanner(tempFile);\n        int total = 0;\n        while (input.hasNext())\n        {\n            total = total + input.nextInt();\n        }\n        input.close();\n        return total;\n    }\n}\nThis class does not compile. Consider the following statements about why it fails and how to fix it.\nI. The class fails to compile because File and IOException are part of java.io and must be imported, but no import statement for java.io is present.\nII. Adding import java.io.*; to the top of the file would resolve this particular compilation error.\nIII. Scanner is also part of java.io, so an additional import java.util.*; statement is unnecessary.\nIV. Declaring throws IOException in the method header, as shown, is unnecessary and should be removed for the class to compile.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, and III only", "I and II only", "II and IV only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Statements I and II are true: File and IOException live in the java.io package and must be imported, most simply with import java.io.*;, which resolves this specific compilation error (4.6.A.5). Statement III is false because Scanner is part of java.util, a different package from java.io, so an import java.util.*; statement (or an equivalent import for Scanner) is still required; statement IV is false because declaring throws IOException is exactly the correct way to handle the checked exception that the Scanner(File) constructor can throw, so it should be kept, not removed, in order for the method to compile correctly (4.6.A.4)."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file attendees.txt contains an unknown number of names, separated by spaces, with no name empty. A programmer wants to count how many names begin with an uppercase letter that comes before 'M' in the alphabet (that is, 'A' through 'L'). Consider the following code segment.\nFile nameFile = new File(\"attendees.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(nameFile);\nint count = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    String name = input.next();\n    char firstLetter = name.charAt(0);\n    if (firstLetter < 'M')\n    {\n        count++;\n    }\n}\ninput.close();\nSystem.out.println(count);\nIf attendees.txt contains the values Kayla Owen Marcus Anna Liam Tessa Ben, what is printed?", "choices": ["3", "5", "4", "7"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Checking each name's first character against 'M' using charAt(0), the names \"Kayla\", \"Anna\", \"Liam\", and \"Ben\" each begin with a letter that comes before 'M' in the alphabet, giving a count of 4; \"Owen\" and \"Tessa\" begin with letters after 'M', and \"Marcus\" begins with 'M' itself, which does not satisfy firstLetter < 'M' (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.9). The other choices miscount which names actually qualify, most commonly by mishandling the boundary case of a name beginning with 'M' itself."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file oddEven.txt contains an unknown number of integers, separated by spaces. A programmer wants to compute evenPositionSum, the sum of only the values found at the 1st, 3rd, 5th, and so on positions in the file (using 1-based counting). Three implementations are shown below, each assuming input is a valid Scanner object connected to oddEven.txt.\nImplementation I\nint evenPositionSum = 0;\nint position = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    int val = input.nextInt();\n    position++;\n    if (position % 2 == 1)\n    {\n        evenPositionSum = evenPositionSum + val;\n    }\n}\nImplementation II\nint evenPositionSum = 0;\nboolean keep = false;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    int val = input.nextInt();\n    if (keep)\n    {\n        evenPositionSum = evenPositionSum + val;\n    }\n    keep = !keep;\n}\nImplementation III\nint evenPositionSum = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    evenPositionSum = evenPositionSum + input.nextInt();\n    input.nextInt();\n}\nWhich of these implementations always correctly computes evenPositionSum, regardless of how many total values the file contains?", "choices": ["II only", "III only", "II and III only", "I only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Implementation I correctly tracks a running position counter and adds a value only when its 1-based position is odd, which works no matter how many total values the file contains (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.9). Implementation II initializes keep to false, so it actually adds the value at position 2, then position 4, and so on, summing the even positions instead of the odd ones; Implementation III always calls a second, unguarded nextInt() after adding a value, so whenever the file contains an odd total number of values, the final call to nextInt() has no token left to consume and throws a NoSuchElementException."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "A programmer writes the following code segment, intending to read and sum every one of the exactly 8 integers contained in the file scoresFixed.txt.\nFile fixedFile = new File(\"scoresFixed.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(fixedFile);\nint sum = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i <= 8; i++)          // Line 4\n{\n    sum = sum + input.nextInt();      // Line 6\n}\nThis code segment throws a NoSuchElementException. Which change would fix the code segment so that it reads exactly the 8 integers in the file without throwing an exception?", "choices": ["In line 4, changing i <= 8 to i < 8", "In line 6, changing input.nextInt() to input.next()", "In line 4, changing the loop condition to i < 9", "In line 4, removing the loop entirely and replacing it with a single call to input.nextInt()"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The loop condition i <= 8 causes the loop to execute 9 times (i equal to 0 through 8), one more time than the file has values for, so the ninth call to nextInt() finds no token left and throws a NoSuchElementException; changing the condition to i < 8 executes the loop exactly 8 times, matching the number of values in the file (4.6.A.9). Changing nextInt() to next() would return Strings instead of ints and would not fix the underlying off-by-one error, changing the condition to i < 9 still produces 9 iterations (i equal to 0 through 8) and does not fix the exception, and removing the loop entirely would read only a single value instead of all 8."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.8", "prompt": "The text file roster2.txt contains the following lines of text, and no other lines.\nDiaz,11\nChen,10\nPatel,12\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from roster2.txt.\nFile rosterFile = new File(\"roster2.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(rosterFile);\nint totalNameLength = 0;\nint lineCount = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    String line = input.nextLine();\n    String[] parts = line.split(\",\");\n    totalNameLength = totalNameLength + parts[0].length();\n    lineCount++;\n}\ninput.close();\nSystem.out.println(totalNameLength + \" \" + lineCount);\nConsider the following statements about this code segment.\nI. The loop calls nextLine() exactly once per iteration, reading one full line of text from the file each time.\nII. split(\",\") divides each line into a two-element array, where parts[0] is the name and parts[1] is the grade, both as Strings.\nIII. The code segment prints \"14 3\", since \"Diaz\", \"Chen\", and \"Patel\" have a combined total of 14 characters across 3 lines.\nIV. This code segment mixes nextLine() with other Scanner token-reading methods, such as next() or nextInt(), on the same Scanner object.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, and III only", "I and II only", "II and IV only", "I, III, and IV only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Statements I and II are true: the loop calls nextLine() once per iteration to read a full line, and split(\",\") produces a two-element array containing the name and grade as separate Strings (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.8). Statement III is false because \"Diaz\", \"Chen\", and \"Patel\" contain 4, 4, and 5 characters respectively, a combined total of 13, not 14, so the code segment actually prints \"13 3\"; statement IV is false because this code segment calls only nextLine() to read from the file and never calls next() or nextInt() on the same Scanner object, so it does not exhibit the mixing pattern described."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file elevations.txt contains an unknown number of integer elevation readings, in feet, separated by spaces. A programmer wants to find both the highest and lowest readings in the file. Consider the following code segment.\nFile elevFile = new File(\"elevations.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(elevFile);\nint highest = input.nextInt();\nint lowest = highest;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    int reading = input.nextInt();\n    if (reading > highest)\n    {\n        highest = reading;\n    }\n    else if (reading < lowest)\n    {\n        lowest = reading;\n    }\n}\ninput.close();\nSystem.out.println(highest + \" \" + lowest);\nIf elevations.txt contains the values 500 1200 300 1200 250 900, what is printed?", "choices": ["1200 300", "1200 500", "1200 250", "900 250"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Initializing both highest and lowest to the first value read (500), the loop then compares each remaining reading using an if/else-if structure: 1200 becomes the new highest, 300 becomes the new lowest, the second 1200 matches the current highest without updating either variable, 250 becomes the new lowest, and 900 changes neither, leaving highest at 1200 and lowest at 250 (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.9). The other choices reflect miscounting which values update highest or lowest, such as stopping at the first new lowest value found or never updating highest and lowest past their initial reading."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.10", "prompt": "A programmer submits the following method, intended to open the file specified by filename, read every integer token in it, and return their sum.\nimport java.io.File;\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport java.util.Scanner;\npublic class DataUtil\n{\n    public static int sumFile(String filename) throws IOException\n    {\n        File dataFile = new File(filename);\n        Scanner input = new Scanner(dataFile);\n        int total = 0;\n        while (input.hasNext())\n        {\n            total = total + input.nextInt();\n        }\n        input.close();\n        return total;\n    }\n}\nConsider the following statements about this method.\nI. The method compiles successfully, since File and IOException are properly imported from java.io and Scanner is properly imported from java.util.\nII. Declaring throws IOException in the method header is necessary because the Scanner(File) constructor can throw a checked IOException.\nIII. If filename refers to a file containing a non-numeric token, such as a word, the method throws an InputMismatchException when it reaches that token.\nIV. Removing the call to input.close() at the end of the method would cause the method to fail to compile.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["II and III only", "I and IV only", "I, II, III, and IV", "I, II, and III only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Statements I, II, and III are all true: the necessary classes are correctly imported from java.io and java.util so the method compiles, declaring throws IOException correctly handles the checked exception that Scanner(File) can throw, and encountering a non-numeric token during a call to nextInt() throws an InputMismatchException, as expected (4.6.A.4, 4.6.A.5, 4.6.A.6). Statement IV is false because omitting input.close() does not prevent the method from compiling; it only means the file resource is not properly released, a runtime resource-management concern rather than a compilation error (4.6.A.10)."},
  {"id": "4.6-complex-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.6", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.6.A.9", "prompt": "The text file ticketCodes.txt contains the following text.\nBB12 AA05 CC20 AA99 BA10 AB50\nIn the following code segment, a valid Scanner object named input is created to read from ticketCodes.txt.\nFile codeFile = new File(\"ticketCodes.txt\");\nScanner input = new Scanner(codeFile);\nint earlyCount = 0;\nwhile (input.hasNext())\n{\n    String code = input.next();\n    if (code.compareTo(\"BA00\") < 0)\n    {\n        earlyCount++;\n    }\n}\ninput.close();\nSystem.out.println(earlyCount);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["3", "2", "4", "6"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Using compareTo, a String is considered \"less than\" another if it comes first alphabetically (comparing character by character), and \"AA05\", \"AA99\", and \"AB50\" all begin with 'A', which precedes 'B', so each has a negative compareTo result against \"BA00\", making earlyCount equal to 3 (4.6.A.6, 4.6.A.9). \"BB12\" and \"CC20\" begin with letters that come after 'B', and \"BA10\" matches \"BA00\" through its first two characters but has a larger third character ('1' versus '0'), so none of these three qualify as \"less than\" \"BA00\"."},
  {"id": "4.7-basic-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.7.A.4", "prompt": "A program reads the String \"152\" from user input and needs to store its value as an int named ticketCount so it can be used in arithmetic. Which statement correctly performs this conversion?", "choices": ["int ticketCount = \"152\";", "int ticketCount = Integer.parseInt(\"152\");", "int ticketCount = (int) \"152\";", "int ticketCount = Double.parseDouble(\"152\");"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Integer.parseInt(String s) is the static method designed to convert a numeric String into an int value (4.7.A.4). Assigning a String directly to an int does not compile, casting a String with (int) is not a valid conversion in Java, and Double.parseDouble returns a double, not an int, so it would not compile when assigned to ticketCount."},
  {"id": "4.7-basic-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.7.A.5", "prompt": "A program reads the String \"98.6\" and needs to store its value as a double named bodyTemp. Which statement correctly performs this conversion?", "choices": ["double bodyTemp = Integer.parseInt(\"98.6\");", "double bodyTemp = \"98.6\";", "double bodyTemp = Double.parseDouble(\"98.6\");", "double bodyTemp = (double) \"98.6\";"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Double.parseDouble(String s) is the static method designed to convert a numeric String into a double value (4.7.A.5). Integer.parseInt cannot process a String containing a decimal point, assigning a String directly to a double does not compile, and casting a String with (double) is not a valid conversion in Java."},
  {"id": "4.7-basic-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.7.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nInteger points = 45;\nWhat allows this statement to compile, even though 45 is an int literal and points is declared as an Integer?", "choices": ["Java automatically unboxes the int value into an Integer object", "The statement does not actually compile", "Integer is treated as a primitive type identical to int", "Java automatically autoboxes the int value into an Integer object"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Autoboxing is the automatic conversion the compiler performs when a primitive value, such as the int literal 45, is assigned to a variable of the corresponding wrapper type, such as Integer (4.7.A.2). Unboxing describes the opposite conversion, from wrapper to primitive, so it does not apply here; the statement compiles successfully; and Integer is a reference type distinct from the primitive int, even though autoboxing lets them interact smoothly."},
  {"id": "4.7-basic-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.7.A.3", "prompt": "Consider the following method header and call.\npublic static double square(double x)\n{\n    return x * x;\n}\nDouble price = 6.0;\nSystem.out.println(square(price));\nWhat allows the call square(price) to compile, even though price is a Double object and square expects a double?", "choices": ["Double and double are the exact same type in Java", "The call does not actually compile", "Java automatically autoboxes price into a Double before the call", "Java automatically unboxes price into a double before the call"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Unboxing is the automatic conversion the compiler performs when a wrapper object, such as the Double object price, is passed as an argument to a parameter expecting the corresponding primitive type, such as double (4.7.A.3). Autoboxing describes the opposite conversion, from primitive to wrapper, so it does not apply here; the call compiles and runs successfully; and Double and double are related but distinct types, one a reference type and one a primitive."},
  {"id": "4.7-basic-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.7.A.1", "prompt": "Which of the following best describes an important property of the Integer class?", "choices": ["An Integer object's value can be changed after it is created by calling a mutator method", "The Integer class is part of java.util and must always be imported", "An Integer object is immutable, meaning its value cannot change once the object is created", "Integer objects can only be created using autoboxing, never with the new keyword"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The Integer class, part of java.lang, produces immutable objects: once an Integer object is created, its stored value cannot be changed (4.7.A.1). There is no mutator method that changes an existing Integer object's value, Integer is part of java.lang rather than java.util and does not require an explicit import, and Integer objects can be created either through autoboxing or, historically, with new."},
  {"id": "4.7-basic-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.7.A.1", "prompt": "Which of the following best describes an important property of the Double class?", "choices": ["A Double object is immutable, meaning its value cannot change once the object is created", "A Double object automatically updates its value whenever the underlying double primitive changes", "The Double class must be manually written by the programmer before it can be used", "A Double object's value can be changed after creation using a method named setValue"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Like Integer, the Double class produces immutable objects: once a Double object is created, its value cannot be changed (4.7.A.1). Double objects do not track or update themselves based on unrelated primitive variables, the Double class is already provided by java.lang and does not need to be written by the programmer, and there is no setValue method that mutates an existing Double object."},
  {"id": "4.7-basic-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.7.A.4", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nString input = \"27\";\nint result = Integer.parseInt(input) + 3;\nWhat value is stored in result after this code executes?", "choices": ["30", "The code does not compile", "\"273\"", "27"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Integer.parseInt(input) converts the String \"27\" into the int value 27, and 27 + 3 evaluates to 30, which is stored in result (4.7.A.4). The code compiles and runs successfully, concatenating the strings \"27\" and \"3\" would only happen if + were applied to Strings rather than to the parsed int, and 27 alone ignores the addition of 3."},
  {"id": "4.7-basic-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.7.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following method header.\npublic static void logScore(Integer score)\n{\n    System.out.println(score);\n}\nWhich of the following method calls compiles because of autoboxing?", "choices": ["logScore(\"95\");", "logScore(95);", "logScore(95.0);", "logScore();"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Passing the int literal 95 to a parameter declared as Integer triggers autoboxing, the automatic conversion from a primitive value to its wrapper type, allowing the call to compile (4.7.A.2). Passing a String does not match the Integer parameter and does not compile, passing 95.0 is a double literal that autoboxes to Double rather than Integer and does not compile, and calling logScore() supplies no argument at all, which does not match the method's single-parameter header."},
  {"id": "4.7-intermediate-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.7.A.3", "prompt": "Consider the following method and code segment.\npublic static double addTax(double amount)\n{\n    return amount * 1.08;\n}\nDouble subtotal = new Double(50.0);\nSystem.out.println(addTax(subtotal));\nWhat is printed when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["The code does not compile, because subtotal is a Double object and addTax expects a double", "0.0", "54.0", "50.0"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Although subtotal is declared as a Double object, Java automatically unboxes it into a primitive double when it is passed to addTax, so the method runs normally with amount equal to 50.0 and returns 50.0 * 1.08, which is 54.0 (4.7.A.3). The code compiles and runs successfully rather than failing, and 50.0 alone would only be correct if no tax were applied."},
  {"id": "4.7-intermediate-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.7.A.4", "prompt": "A program stores quiz scores as Strings in an array named rawScores, such as {\"88\", \"91\", \"79\"}. A programmer wants to compute the sum of these scores as an int named total. Which code segment correctly computes this sum?", "choices": ["int total = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < rawScores.length; i++)\n{\n    total = total + rawScores[i];\n}", "int total = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < rawScores.length; i++)\n{\n    total = total + Integer.parseInt(rawScores[i]);\n}", "int total = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < rawScores.length; i++)\n{\n    total = total + Double.parseDouble(rawScores[i]);\n}", "int total = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < rawScores.length; i++)\n{\n    total = total + (int) rawScores[i];\n}"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Each element of rawScores is a String, so Integer.parseInt(rawScores[i]) must be used to convert it to an int before it can be added to total (4.7.A.4). Adding a String directly to an int does not compile, Double.parseDouble returns a double, which cannot be added directly into an int variable without an additional conversion, and casting a String with (int) is not a valid Java conversion."},
  {"id": "4.7-intermediate-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.7.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\npublic static void printDouble(Integer value)\n{\n    System.out.println(value * 2);\n}\nint quantity = 12;\nprintDouble(quantity);\nWhich statement correctly explains why this code compiles?", "choices": ["The code does not compile, because quantity is an int and printDouble expects an Integer", "Java automatically unboxes quantity into an Integer before the call", "Java automatically autoboxes quantity into an Integer before the call", "int and Integer are treated as identical types by the compiler"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Passing the primitive int quantity to a parameter declared as the wrapper type Integer triggers autoboxing, the automatic conversion applied when a primitive value is passed as a parameter expecting the wrapper type (4.7.A.2). The code compiles and runs successfully, unboxing describes the opposite conversion from wrapper to primitive and does not apply here, and int and Integer remain distinct types even though autoboxing lets them interoperate."},
  {"id": "4.7-intermediate-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.7.A.5", "prompt": "A program reads a line of user input representing a temperature, such as \"101.4\", and needs to determine whether the temperature exceeds 100.0. Which code segment correctly performs this check?", "choices": ["String reading = \"101.4\";\nif (reading > 100.0)\n{\n    System.out.println(\"Fever\");\n}", "String reading = \"101.4\";\nif (Integer.parseInt(reading) > 100.0)\n{\n    System.out.println(\"Fever\");\n}", "String reading = \"101.4\";\nif (reading.parseDouble() > 100.0)\n{\n    System.out.println(\"Fever\");\n}", "String reading = \"101.4\";\nif (Double.parseDouble(reading) > 100.0)\n{\n    System.out.println(\"Fever\");\n}"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Double.parseDouble(reading) converts the String \"101.4\" into the double value 101.4, which can then be correctly compared to 100.0 (4.7.A.5). Comparing a String directly to a double with > does not compile, Integer.parseInt cannot process a String containing a decimal point and would throw an exception at run time, and parseDouble is a static method of the Double class, not an instance method that can be called on a String object like reading.parseDouble()."},
  {"id": "4.7-intermediate-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.7.A.1", "prompt": "A student writes the following code segment, believing it will change the value stored in an Integer object.\nInteger balance = 100;\nbalance = balance + 50;\nSystem.out.println(balance);\nWhich statement correctly explains what actually happens?", "choices": ["The original Integer object storing 100 is mutated in place to store 150", "The statement does not compile, because Integer objects cannot be used in arithmetic", "A brand-new Integer object storing 150 is created, and balance is reassigned to refer to it, since Integer objects are immutable", "balance still refers to the original Integer object, which now incorrectly stores 100"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Because Integer objects are immutable, the expression balance + 50 unboxes balance's value, computes 150, and autoboxes that new value into a brand-new Integer object, which balance is then reassigned to refer to; the original object storing 100 is left unchanged (4.7.A.1, 4.7.A.2, 4.7.A.3). The code compiles and runs successfully, no existing Integer object is ever mutated in place, and balance correctly ends up referring to an object storing 150, not the stale value 100."},
  {"id": "4.7-intermediate-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.7.A.4", "prompt": "A program reads a token representing a warehouse item count, such as \"340\", using a Scanner named scan.\nint itemCount = Integer.parseInt(scan.next());\nWhich statement best explains why Integer.parseInt is necessary here?", "choices": ["scan.next() already returns an int, so Integer.parseInt only improves performance", "scan.next() returns a String, and directly assigning a String to an int variable does not compile, so the String must be parsed into an int", "Integer.parseInt is only needed if the token contains a decimal point", "scan.next() returns a double, so Integer.parseInt converts it down to an int"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The next() method of Scanner returns a String token, and assigning a String directly to an int variable does not compile, so Integer.parseInt(String s) is required to convert the token into an int value (4.7.A.4). scan.next() does not return an int or a double, so the remaining choices misdescribe what next() actually returns, and parsing is required regardless of whether the token happens to contain a decimal point, since this token, \"340\", does not."},
  {"id": "4.7-intermediate-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.7.A.3", "prompt": "Consider the following method and code segment.\npublic static boolean isPassing(int grade)\n{\n    return grade >= 60;\n}\nInteger finalGrade = 72;\nSystem.out.println(isPassing(finalGrade));\nWhat is printed when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["72", "The code does not compile, because finalGrade is an Integer and isPassing expects an int", "false", "true"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Java automatically unboxes the Integer object finalGrade into the primitive int value 72 when it is passed to isPassing, so the method evaluates 72 >= 60 and returns true, which is what gets printed (4.7.A.3). The code compiles and runs successfully rather than failing, false would only result if the comparison were reversed or the value were below 60, and 72 alone ignores that isPassing returns a boolean rather than the grade itself."},
  {"id": "4.7-intermediate-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.7.A.5", "prompt": "A vending machine program reads the String \"1.75\" representing a snack's price and needs to store it as a double named price so it can be used in a calculation. Two candidate statements are proposed.\nStatement 1\ndouble price = Double.parseDouble(\"1.75\");\nStatement 2\ndouble price = Integer.parseInt(\"1.75\");\nWhich statement correctly evaluates these two candidates?", "choices": ["Statement 1 compiles and correctly stores 1.75; Statement 2 throws a run-time exception because \"1.75\" is not a valid integer format", "Both statements compile and correctly store 1.75 in price", "Statement 1 throws a run-time exception, but Statement 2 compiles and correctly stores 1.75", "Neither statement compiles"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Double.parseDouble(\"1.75\") correctly parses a String containing a decimal point into the double value 1.75, so Statement 1 works as intended (4.7.A.5). Integer.parseInt is designed only for Strings representing whole numbers, so passing it \"1.75\", which contains a decimal point, causes a NumberFormatException at run time rather than compiling into a usable int; Statement 1 does not throw an exception, and Statement 2 does not succeed."},
  {"id": "4.7-complex-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.7.A.1", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nInteger scoreA = 90;\nInteger scoreB = 90;\nInteger scoreC = Integer.parseInt(\"500\");\nInteger scoreD = Integer.parseInt(\"500\");\nSystem.out.println(scoreA == scoreB);\nSystem.out.println(scoreC == scoreD);\nSystem.out.println(scoreC.equals(scoreD));\nWhat is printed when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["true\ntrue\ntrue", "false\nfalse\ntrue", "true\nfalse\ntrue", "false\ntrue\ntrue"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Java caches Integer objects for values from -128 to 127, so scoreA and scoreB, both autoboxed from 90, refer to the same cached object and scoreA == scoreB is true; scoreC and scoreD, both storing 500, fall outside that cached range and are each backed by separate Integer objects, so scoreC == scoreD compares references and is false, while scoreC.equals(scoreD) correctly compares their stored values and is true (4.7.A.1, 4.7.A.2). Any answer that reports scoreA == scoreB as false, or reports scoreC == scoreD as true, misunderstands how Integer caching affects reference equality outside the small cached range."},
  {"id": "4.7-complex-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.7.A.1", "prompt": "A student writes the following method, intending it to double the value stored in an Integer object passed as a parameter.\npublic static void doubleValue(Integer num)\n{\n    num = num * 2;\n}\nInteger quantity = 40;\ndoubleValue(quantity);\nSystem.out.println(quantity);\nConsider the following statements about this code.\nI. The method compiles successfully.\nII. When doubleValue runs, num is unboxed, multiplied by 2, and the result is autoboxed into a brand-new Integer object that num is reassigned to reference.\nIII. Because Integer objects are immutable, no method can ever change the value quantity refers to through reassignment inside num.\nIV. The code prints 40, because reassigning the parameter num inside the method has no effect on the caller's variable quantity.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II, III, and IV", "I, II, and IV only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Statements I, II, and IV are true: the method compiles, num is unboxed, doubled, and reassigned to a new Integer object created by autoboxing, and since num is a local copy of the reference, reassigning it never affects quantity in the caller, so 40 is printed (4.7.A.1, 4.7.A.2, 4.7.A.3). Statement III overstates the point: while it is true that Integer objects are immutable, that is not why quantity is unaffected here; quantity is unaffected because Java passes a copy of the reference to num, so reassigning num alone could never change quantity even if Integer objects were mutable."},
  {"id": "4.7-complex-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.7.A.4", "prompt": "A program reads ticket-price tokens from a line of a data file named tickets.txt, one per ticket type, and needs to store each as an int in an array named prices. A single line contains three space-separated tokens, such as \"12 18 25\". Consider the following code segment.\nFile dataFile = new File(\"tickets.txt\");\nScanner scan = new Scanner(dataFile);\nint[] prices = new int[3];\nfor (int i = 0; i < prices.length; i++)\n{\n    prices[i] = Integer.parseInt(scan.next());\n}\nConsider the following statements about this code segment.\nI. The code compiles successfully.\nII. scan.next() returns a String representing one token at a time from the input line.\nIII. Integer.parseInt converts each String token into an int before it is stored in the prices array.\nIV. If one of the tokens were \"18.5\" instead of a whole number, Integer.parseInt would throw a run-time exception when processing that token.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I and III only", "II and IV only", "I, II, and III only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "All four statements are true: the code compiles, scan.next() reads one whitespace-delimited String token at a time, Integer.parseInt converts each token to an int before it is stored in prices, and Integer.parseInt cannot process a String containing a decimal point like \"18.5\", so it would throw a NumberFormatException at run time if it encountered that token (4.7.A.4). Because every statement is accurate, any option omitting one of them is incomplete."},
  {"id": "4.7-complex-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.7.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following overloaded methods, both named report, in the same class.\npublic static void report(int x)\n{\n    System.out.println(\"int version: \" + x);\n}\npublic static void report(Integer x)\n{\n    System.out.println(\"Integer version: \" + x);\n}\nConsider the following statements about calls made from within the same class.\nI. report(25) calls the int version, because Java prefers an exact primitive match over autoboxing when both overloads are available.\nII. report(Integer.valueOf(25)) calls the Integer version, because the argument is already a wrapper object.\nIII. Autoboxing occurs when report(25) is called, since 25 must first be autoboxed before either version can be selected.\nIV. If only the Integer version of report existed, calling report(25) would still compile, because Java would autobox 25 into an Integer.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, and IV only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Statements I, II, and IV are true: when both overloads exist, Java prefers the exact primitive match for report(25), an already-boxed Integer argument matches the Integer overload directly, and if only the Integer version existed, report(25) would still compile because autoboxing would convert 25 into an Integer to match it (4.7.A.2). Statement III is false because autoboxing is not needed at all when both overloads are available and report(25) is called, since the int version already matches the primitive argument exactly without any conversion."},
  {"id": "4.7-complex-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.7.A.5", "prompt": "A program reads a line representing a shipment's weight, such as \"48.25\", and a separate program reads a line representing a shipment count, such as \"48\". A student writes the following code segment intending to add these two values together as a single double.\nString weightText = \"48.25\";\nString countText = \"48\";\ndouble combinedTotal = Double.parseDouble(weightText) + Integer.parseInt(countText);\nConsider the following statements about this code segment.\nI. The code compiles successfully.\nII. Double.parseDouble(weightText) evaluates to the double value 48.25.\nIII. Integer.parseInt(countText) evaluates to the int value 48, which is then implicitly widened to a double before the addition occurs.\nIV. The value stored in combinedTotal is 96.25.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, and III only", "I, II, III, and IV", "II and IV only", "I and IV only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "All four statements are true: the code compiles, Double.parseDouble(weightText) evaluates to 48.25, Integer.parseInt(countText) evaluates to the int 48 which Java widens to a double before adding it to a double operand, and 48.25 + 48.0 correctly evaluates to 96.25, which is stored in combinedTotal (4.7.A.4, 4.7.A.5). Since every statement is accurate, any option that omits one of them is incomplete."},
  {"id": "4.7-complex-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.7.A.1", "prompt": "A student writes the following method, intending it to reset an Integer field named attempts back to zero.\npublic class Counter\n{\n    private Integer attempts;\n    public Counter(int start)\n    {\n        attempts = start;\n    }\n    public void reset()\n    {\n        attempts = 0;\n    }\n    public int getAttempts()\n    {\n        return attempts;\n    }\n}\nConsider the following statements about this class.\nI. The class compiles successfully, including the assignment attempts = 0; inside reset.\nII. The statement attempts = 0; does not mutate the existing Integer object that attempts previously referenced; instead, it autoboxes 0 into a new Integer object and reassigns attempts to reference it.\nIII. Because Integer objects are immutable, no instance variable of type Integer can ever be reassigned after the constructor runs.\nIV. The method getAttempts compiles successfully because Java automatically unboxes attempts when it is returned as an int.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, and IV only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Statements I, II, and IV are true: the class compiles, reset works by autoboxing 0 into a brand-new Integer object and reassigning the attempts field to it rather than mutating the original object, and getAttempts compiles because attempts is unboxed automatically when returned as an int (4.7.A.1, 4.7.A.2, 4.7.A.3). Statement III is false: immutability means an Integer object's own stored value cannot change, but it does not prevent a variable or field of type Integer from being reassigned to reference a different Integer object entirely, which is exactly what reset does."},
  {"id": "4.7-complex-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.7.A.3", "prompt": "Consider the following overloaded methods in the same class.\npublic static String classify(double x)\n{\n    return \"double version\";\n}\npublic static String classify(Double x)\n{\n    return \"Double version\";\n}\nConsider the following statements about calls made from within the same class.\nDouble reading = new Double(3.5);\nSystem.out.println(classify(reading));\nI. The call classify(reading) compiles successfully.\nII. classify(reading) invokes the Double version, since reading is already a Double object and matches that overload directly without requiring any conversion.\nIII. classify(reading) invokes the double version, because Java always unboxes a Double argument before a method call.\nIV. If only the version classify(double x) existed, calling classify(reading) would still compile, because Java would unbox reading into a double to match it.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, and IV only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Statements I, II, and IV are true: the call compiles, an already-boxed Double argument matches the Double overload directly without needing unboxing, and if only the primitive-parameter version existed, the call would still compile because Java would unbox reading into a double to match it (4.7.A.3). Statement III is false because Java does not always unbox a wrapper argument before a call; when an exact reference-type overload is available, as it is here, Java prefers that exact match over performing an unboxing conversion."},
  {"id": "4.7-complex-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.7", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.7.A.1", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nInteger idA = 100;\nInteger idB = 100;\nInteger idC = 300;\nInteger idD = 300;\nSystem.out.println(idA == idB);\nSystem.out.println(idC == idD);\nA student claims that because both pairs of variables are declared and assigned in exactly the same way, both println statements must print the same boolean value. Which statement best evaluates this claim?", "choices": ["The claim is correct, because Integer objects are always cached regardless of their value, so every Integer variable holding an equal value refers to the same object", "The claim is incorrect: idA == idB prints true because 100 falls within Java's cached Integer range of -128 to 127, so idA and idB reference the same cached object, while idC == idD prints false because 300 falls outside that range, so idC and idD reference two separate objects with equal values but different references", "The claim is correct, because == always compares the actual int values stored inside Integer objects, never their references", "The claim is incorrect: idA == idB prints false and idC == idD prints true, since larger cached values take priority over smaller ones"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Java automatically caches Integer objects for values from -128 to 127, so idA and idB, both storing 100, refer to the identical cached object and idA == idB is true; 300 falls outside that cached range, so idC and idD are backed by two distinct objects created independently, making idC == idD compare different references and print false, even though both objects store equal values (4.7.A.1, 4.7.A.2). The claim that caching applies to all values, that == compares stored values rather than references for Integer objects, and that larger values are cached in preference to smaller ones are all incorrect descriptions of how Integer caching and == actually behave."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.3", "prompt": "A programmer wants to declare and construct an ArrayList that will store only String objects. Which of the following statements accomplishes this correctly?", "choices": ["ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<String>();", "ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<Integer>();", "String ArrayList names = new String ArrayList();", "ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>(String);"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Declaring names as ArrayList<String> and constructing it with new ArrayList<String>() correctly creates an ArrayList that is restricted to holding String objects, letting the compiler catch type errors (4.8.A.3). The second statement fails to compile because the generic type on the constructor (Integer) does not match the declared type (String); the third statement uses invalid Java syntax, since ArrayList must appear as a single class name; the fourth statement incorrectly passes String as a constructor argument rather than placing it inside angle brackets as the generic type."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.4", "prompt": "A student wants to use the ArrayList class in a Java program. Which of the following must appear at the top of the file for the code to compile?", "choices": ["import java.lang.ArrayList;", "import java.util.ArrayList;", "import java.util.List;", "No import statement is required, since ArrayList is part of the default package."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "ArrayList is part of the java.util package, so import java.util.ArrayList; must appear before the class can be used (4.8.A.4). java.lang is the package that is imported automatically and does not contain ArrayList; importing java.util.List only makes the List interface name available and does not by itself give access to the ArrayList class name; and ArrayList is not part of any package that is available without an import."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\npets.add(\"Fido\");\npets.add(\"Milo\");\npets.add(\"Rex\");\nWhat value is returned by the call pets.size(), assuming pets was constructed as an empty ArrayList<String> before this code segment executed?", "choices": ["2", "1", "3", "4"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The size() method returns the number of elements currently stored in the list; since three String values were added and none were removed, pets.size() returns 3 (4.8.A.5). The other choices count too few or too many elements, likely from miscounting the add calls or assuming size() reflects some other quantity such as capacity."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.6", "prompt": "An ArrayList named colors currently contains 5 elements. Which of the following is the index of the last element in colors?", "choices": ["5", "6", "colors.length", "colors.size() - 1"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "ArrayList indices run from 0 to size() - 1, so with 5 elements the last valid index is colors.size() - 1, which evaluates to 4 (4.8.A.6). The value 5 is one past the last valid index and would throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException; 6 is even further out of range; and colors.length is not valid syntax for an ArrayList, since length is a field used with arrays, not a method or field of the ArrayList class."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.1", "prompt": "Which of the following best describes a key property of an ArrayList object, in contrast to an array?", "choices": ["An ArrayList can grow or shrink in size as elements are added or removed, while an array has a fixed length once created.", "An ArrayList must be declared using a primitive element type, such as int or double.", "An ArrayList automatically sorts its elements every time a new element is added.", "An ArrayList can only store String objects."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "An ArrayList object is mutable in size, meaning elements can be added or removed and the list adjusts accordingly, unlike an array, whose length is fixed once it is created (4.8.A.1). ArrayLists must use reference types rather than primitive types for their generic type parameter; adding an element never triggers automatic sorting; and an ArrayList can store any reference type, not only String."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nscores.add(70);\nscores.add(85);\nscores.add(93);\nWhat value is returned by the call scores.get(1), assuming scores was constructed as an empty ArrayList<Integer> before this code segment executed?", "choices": ["70", "85", "93", "An IndexOutOfBoundsException is thrown"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "After the three add calls, scores contains [70, 85, 93], and get(1) returns the element at index 1, which is 85 (4.8.A.5, 4.8.A.6). 70 is the element at index 0, 93 is the element at index 2, and index 1 is a valid index for a list of size 3, so no exception is thrown."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nteam.add(\"Ravens\");\nteam.add(\"Wolves\");\nString previous = team.set(1, \"Tigers\");\nWhat value is stored in previous after this code segment executes, assuming team was constructed as an empty ArrayList<String> before this code segment executed?", "choices": ["\"Ravens\"", "\"Tigers\"", "\"Wolves\"", "null"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The set(int index, E obj) method replaces the element at the given index with the new value but returns the OLD value that was previously stored there; since index 1 held \"Wolves\" before the call, that is the value returned and stored in previous (4.8.A.5). \"Ravens\" is the element at index 0 and is unaffected by this call; \"Tigers\" is the new value being stored into the list, not the value returned; and null would only be returned if index 1 had never been assigned a value."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nnums.add(4);\nnums.add(8);\nnums.add(12);\nint removedValue = nums.remove(1);\nWhat value is stored in removedValue after this code segment executes, assuming nums was constructed as an empty ArrayList<Integer> before this code segment executed?", "choices": ["4", "1", "12", "8"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The remove(int index) method removes the element at the given index and returns the REMOVED value; since index 1 in [4, 8, 12] holds 8, that is the value removed and returned (4.8.A.5). 4 and 12 are the elements at indices 0 and 2, which remain in the list, and 1 is simply the index argument, not a value stored in the list."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nline.add(\"Ann\");\nline.add(\"Ben\");\nline.add(\"Cody\");\nWhat are the contents of line after this code segment executes, assuming line was constructed as an empty ArrayList<String> before this code segment executed?", "choices": ["[\"Ann\", \"Ben\", \"Cody\"]", "[\"Cody\", \"Ben\", \"Ann\"]", "[\"Ben\", \"Ann\", \"Cody\"]", "[\"Ann\", \"Cody\", \"Ben\"]"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The single-argument add(E obj) method always appends the given value to the end of the list, so calling it three times in this order builds the list in exactly the order the values were added: [\"Ann\", \"Ben\", \"Cody\"] (4.8.A.5). The other choices reorder the names in ways that would only occur if add(int index, E obj) had been used to insert at specific positions, which did not happen here."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nnums.add(10);\nnums.add(20);\nnums.add(1, 15);\nWhat are the contents of nums after this code segment executes, assuming nums was constructed as an empty ArrayList<Integer> before this code segment executed?", "choices": ["[15, 10, 20]", "[10, 15, 20]", "[10, 20, 15]", "[15, 20, 10]"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "After the first two calls, nums contains [10, 20]; the call add(1, 15) inserts 15 at index 1, shifting the element previously at index 1 (20) one position to the right, producing [10, 15, 20] (4.8.A.5). The other choices either place 15 at the wrong position or incorrectly rearrange 10 and 20, which does not happen since add(int, E) only shifts elements at the insertion index and higher."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.3", "prompt": "A student attempts to declare the following ArrayList.\nArrayList<int> values = new ArrayList<int>();\nWhy does this statement fail to compile?", "choices": ["ArrayList objects cannot be declared using the new keyword.", "The variable name values conflicts with a reserved keyword in Java.", "The generic type used in an ArrayList<E> declaration must be a reference type, and int is a primitive type, so Integer must be used instead.", "ArrayList declarations must always use a String as the generic type."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Because the generic type E in ArrayList<E> must be a reference type, a primitive type like int cannot be used; the wrapper class Integer must be used instead, as in ArrayList<Integer> (4.8.A.3). ArrayList objects are normally constructed with new, so that is not the issue; values is not a reserved keyword; and ArrayList is not restricted to only String as its generic type, since ArrayList<Integer>, ArrayList<Double>, and other reference types are equally valid."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.2", "prompt": "What are the contents of the ArrayList created by the following statement, immediately after it executes?\nArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<String>();", "choices": ["[null]", "[\"\"]", "[0]", "An empty list containing no elements"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The no-argument ArrayList() constructor always constructs an empty list, meaning size() returns 0 and there are no elements at all, not even placeholder elements (4.8.A.2). The other choices incorrectly assume the list starts with one element already stored, whether null, an empty String, or 0, but a newly constructed ArrayList genuinely contains zero elements until add is called."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nprices.add(2.50);\nprices.add(4.75);\ndouble first = prices.get(0);\nWhat value is stored in first after this code segment executes, assuming prices was constructed as an empty ArrayList<Double> before this code segment executed?", "choices": ["2.5", "4.75", "0.0", "An IndexOutOfBoundsException is thrown"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "After the two add calls, prices contains [2.50, 4.75], and get(0) returns the element at index 0, which is 2.5 (4.8.A.5, 4.8.A.6). 4.75 is the element at index 1, 0.0 would only apply if the list defaulted its stored elements, which ArrayLists do not do since they start empty, and index 0 is valid for a list of size 2, so no exception occurs."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.6", "prompt": "An ArrayList named letters contains exactly 3 elements. Which of the following statements will cause an IndexOutOfBoundsException when executed?", "choices": ["letters.get(0);", "letters.get(3);", "letters.get(2);", "letters.get(1);"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Valid indices for a list of size 3 run from 0 to 2 (size() - 1), so letters.get(3) references an index that does not exist and throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException (4.8.A.6). Indices 0, 1, and 2 are all within the valid range and will not cause an exception."},
  {"id": "4.8-basic-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nqueue.add(\"Amir\");\nqueue.add(\"Beth\");\nqueue.add(\"Cho\");\nqueue.remove(0);\nWhat are the contents of queue after this code segment executes, assuming queue was constructed as an empty ArrayList<String> before this code segment executed?", "choices": ["[\"Amir\", \"Beth\", \"Cho\"]", "[\"Beth\", \"Amir\", \"Cho\"]", "[\"Beth\", \"Cho\"]", "[\"Amir\", \"Cho\"]"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "After the three add calls, queue contains [\"Amir\", \"Beth\", \"Cho\"]; removing the element at index 0 (\"Amir\") shifts the remaining elements left by one, producing [\"Beth\", \"Cho\"] with a new size of 2 (4.8.A.5). The first choice ignores that a removal occurred, the second incorrectly reorders elements instead of removing one, and the fourth removes the wrong element."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nnums.add(5);\nnums.add(9);\nnums.add(0, 2);\nnums.set(2, 7);\nnums.add(1);\nWhat are the contents of nums after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[2, 5, 7, 1]", "[2, 5, 9, 1]", "[5, 2, 7, 1]", "[2, 7, 5, 1]"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Tracing step by step: add(5) gives [5]; add(9) gives [5, 9]; add(0, 2) inserts 2 at index 0, giving [2, 5, 9]; set(2, 7) replaces index 2 (9) with 7, giving [2, 5, 7]; add(1) appends 1 to the end, giving [2, 5, 7, 1] (4.8.A.5). The other choices either skip the set call's effect, fail to shift elements correctly during the insertion at index 0, or misplace the appended 1."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> items = new ArrayList<String>();\nitems.add(\"pen\");\nitems.add(\"cup\");\nitems.add(\"book\");\nitems.add(\"lamp\");\nitems.remove(1);\nitems.add(1, \"vase\");\nWhat are the contents of items after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[\"pen\", \"cup\", \"vase\", \"book\", \"lamp\"]", "[\"pen\", \"vase\", \"book\", \"lamp\"]", "[\"pen\", \"book\", \"vase\", \"lamp\"]", "[\"vase\", \"pen\", \"book\", \"lamp\"]"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "After the four add calls, items contains [\"pen\", \"cup\", \"book\", \"lamp\"]; remove(1) removes \"cup\" and shifts the rest left, giving [\"pen\", \"book\", \"lamp\"]; add(1, \"vase\") then inserts \"vase\" at index 1, shifting \"book\" and \"lamp\" right, giving [\"pen\", \"vase\", \"book\", \"lamp\"] (4.8.A.5). The other choices either forget that \"cup\" was already removed before the insertion, insert \"vase\" at the wrong position, or shift elements in the wrong direction."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "A programmer wants the ArrayList letters, which starts empty as an ArrayList<String>, to contain [\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"] after execution. Which of the following code segments produces this result?", "choices": ["letters.add(\"y\");\nletters.add(\"x\");\nletters.add(\"z\");", "letters.add(\"x\");\nletters.add(\"z\");\nletters.add(0, \"y\");", "letters.add(\"x\");\nletters.add(\"y\");\nletters.add(\"z\");", "letters.add(\"x\");\nletters.add(\"z\");\nletters.add(\"y\");"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Calling add(\"x\"), then add(\"y\"), then add(\"z\") appends each value to the end of the list in order, producing exactly [\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"] (4.8.A.5). The first choice adds \"y\" before \"x\", producing [\"y\", \"x\", \"z\"]; the second choice inserts \"y\" at index 0, producing [\"y\", \"x\", \"z\"]; and the fourth choice adds \"z\" before \"y\", producing [\"x\", \"z\", \"y\"], none of which match the target list."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> vals = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nvals.add(3);\nvals.add(6);\nvals.add(9);\nvals.add(0, 1);\nvals.set(2, 8);\nvals.remove(3);\nWhat are the contents of vals after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[1, 3, 8, 9]", "[1, 3, 6, 8]", "[3, 1, 8, 9]", "[1, 3, 8]"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Tracing step by step: after the first three calls vals is [3, 6, 9]; add(0, 1) gives [1, 3, 6, 9]; set(2, 8) replaces index 2 (6) with 8, giving [1, 3, 8, 9]; remove(3) removes index 3 (9), giving [1, 3, 8] (4.8.A.5). The other choices either forget the final remove call, replace the wrong element with set, or shift 1 and 3 out of order."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.1", "prompt": "A programmer needs to store a collection of exam scores but does not know in advance how many scores will need to be stored, since scores are added one at a time as students finish. Which best explains why an ArrayList is a more appropriate choice than an array for this task?", "choices": ["An ArrayList can grow dynamically as elements are added, while an array's length is fixed once it is created.", "An ArrayList provides faster element access than an array.", "An ArrayList requires less memory per element than an array.", "An ArrayList automatically removes duplicate scores."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Because the number of scores is not known in advance, an ArrayList is preferable since it is mutable in size and can grow as new scores are added, unlike an array whose length must be fixed at creation (4.8.A.1). Arrays, not ArrayLists, generally provide faster direct element access; ArrayLists do not use less memory per element than arrays; and neither structure automatically removes duplicate values."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "A student writes the following code segment, intending to change the value at index 2 of an ArrayList to 50.\nArrayList<Integer> data = new ArrayList<Integer>();\ndata.add(10);\ndata.add(20);\ndata.add(30);\ndata[2] = 50;\nWhat is the result of attempting to compile this code segment?", "choices": ["The code compiles and correctly changes index 2 to 50.", "The code does not compile, because square-bracket syntax cannot be used to access or modify elements of an ArrayList; the set method must be used instead.", "The code compiles, but changes index 3 instead of index 2.", "The code compiles, but silently does nothing."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "ArrayList does not support square-bracket indexing the way an array does; only the get and set methods can be used to read or modify its elements, so data[2] = 50; fails to compile, and the statement data.set(2, 50); should be used instead (4.8.A.5). The code cannot compile as written, so none of the outcomes describing successful or silent execution can occur."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> playlist = new ArrayList<String>();\nplaylist.add(\"Song A\");\nplaylist.add(\"Song B\");\nplaylist.add(\"Song C\");\nplaylist.add(2, \"Song D\");\nplaylist.remove(0);\nWhat are the contents of playlist after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[\"Song B\", \"Song C\", \"Song D\"]", "[\"Song A\", \"Song B\", \"Song D\"]", "[\"Song B\", \"Song D\", \"Song C\"]", "[\"Song D\", \"Song B\", \"Song C\"]"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "After the three add calls, playlist is [\"Song A\", \"Song B\", \"Song C\"]; add(2, \"Song D\") inserts \"Song D\" at index 2, shifting \"Song C\" right, giving [\"Song A\", \"Song B\", \"Song D\", \"Song C\"]; remove(0) then removes \"Song A\" and shifts the rest left, giving [\"Song B\", \"Song D\", \"Song C\"] (4.8.A.5). The other choices either forget the insertion at index 2 occurred before the removal, or remove the wrong element."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "A programmer wants the ArrayList counts, which starts empty as an ArrayList<Integer>, to contain [4, 1, 4, 7] after execution. Which of the following code segments produces this result?", "choices": ["counts.add(4);\ncounts.add(1);\ncounts.add(4);\ncounts.add(0, 7);", "counts.add(7);\ncounts.add(4);\ncounts.add(1);\ncounts.add(0, 4);", "counts.add(4);\ncounts.add(7);\ncounts.add(1);\ncounts.add(1, 4);", "counts.add(4);\ncounts.add(1);\ncounts.add(4);\ncounts.add(7);"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Calling add(4), add(1), add(4), and add(7) in order appends each value to the end of the list in turn, producing exactly [4, 1, 4, 7] (4.8.A.5). The first choice inserts 7 at index 0 after building [4, 1, 4], producing [7, 4, 1, 4]; the second choice builds [7, 4, 1] and inserts 4 at index 0, producing [4, 7, 4, 1]; and the third choice builds [4, 7, 1] and inserts 4 at index 1, producing [4, 4, 7, 1], none of which match the target."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Double> temps = new ArrayList<Double>();\ntemps.add(68.0);\ntemps.add(72.0);\ntemps.add(75.0);\ntemps.set(0, 70.0);\ntemps.set(1, temps.get(2));\nWhat are the contents of temps after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[70.0, 75.0, 75.0]", "[70.0, 72.0, 75.0]", "[68.0, 75.0, 75.0]", "[70.0, 75.0, 72.0]"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "After the three add calls, temps is [68.0, 72.0, 75.0]; set(0, 70.0) replaces index 0, giving [70.0, 72.0, 75.0]; set(1, temps.get(2)) reads the current value at index 2 (75.0) and stores it at index 1, giving [70.0, 75.0, 75.0] (4.8.A.5). The other choices either skip the first set call, evaluate temps.get(2) using an outdated list state, or misplace the copied value."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> ranks = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nranks.add(50);\nranks.add(60);\nranks.add(70);\nranks.add(80);\nint last = ranks.size() - 1;\nranks.add(0, ranks.get(last));\nranks.remove(last + 1);\nWhat are the contents of ranks after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[50, 60, 70, 80]", "[80, 50, 60, 70]", "[80, 80, 50, 60, 70]", "[80, 50, 60, 70, 80]"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "After the four add calls, ranks is [50, 60, 70, 80] and last is set to 3 before any further changes; add(0, ranks.get(3)) inserts a second copy of 80 at index 0, giving [80, 50, 60, 70, 80], a list of size 5; remove(last + 1), which is remove(4), then removes the element at index 4, the trailing 80 that was originally the last element, leaving [80, 50, 60, 70] (4.8.A.5, 4.8.A.6). The other choices either forget that last was computed before the insertion or miscount which 80 gets removed."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> waitlist = new ArrayList<String>();\nwaitlist.add(\"Ana\");\nwaitlist.add(\"Bo\");\nwaitlist.add(\"Cai\");\nwaitlist.remove(1);\nwaitlist.add(\"Dee\");\nwaitlist.add(1, \"Eli\");\nWhat are the contents of waitlist after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[\"Ana\", \"Cai\", \"Eli\", \"Dee\"]", "[\"Ana\", \"Bo\", \"Eli\", \"Dee\"]", "[\"Ana\", \"Eli\", \"Cai\", \"Dee\"]", "[\"Eli\", \"Ana\", \"Cai\", \"Dee\"]"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "After the three add calls, waitlist is [\"Ana\", \"Bo\", \"Cai\"]; remove(1) removes \"Bo\", giving [\"Ana\", \"Cai\"]; add(\"Dee\") appends to the end, giving [\"Ana\", \"Cai\", \"Dee\"]; add(1, \"Eli\") inserts \"Eli\" at index 1, shifting \"Cai\" and \"Dee\" right, giving [\"Ana\", \"Eli\", \"Cai\", \"Dee\"] (4.8.A.5). The other choices either forget that \"Bo\" was removed first, place \"Eli\" at the wrong index, or place \"Eli\" before \"Ana\" instead of after it."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<StringBuilder> notes stores a reference to a StringBuilder object. After that reference is added to notes, a separate variable that still refers to the same StringBuilder object calls a mutator method that changes its contents. What effect does this have on the element stored in notes?", "choices": ["No effect; the ArrayList automatically stores an independent copy of every object added to it.", "The ArrayList entry becomes null, since the original reference was modified.", "A compile-time error occurs, since an ArrayList cannot store mutable objects.", "The element retrieved from notes reflects the change, since the ArrayList stores a reference to the same object rather than a copy of it."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "An ArrayList contains object references, not independent copies of objects, so if the same StringBuilder object is modified through any reference to it, retrieving the element from notes will reflect that change (4.8.A.1). The ArrayList does not automatically copy objects, changing an object's contents does not set a reference to null, and ArrayLists can freely store mutable reference types like StringBuilder."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> box = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nbox.add(11);\nbox.add(22);\nbox.add(33);\nbox.set(1, 99);\nbox.add(0, box.get(2));\nWhat are the contents of box after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[33, 11, 99, 33]", "[11, 99, 33, 33]", "[33, 11, 22, 33]", "[11, 99, 33]"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "After the three add calls, box is [11, 22, 33]; set(1, 99) replaces index 1 (22) with 99, giving [11, 99, 33]; add(0, box.get(2)) reads the current value at index 2 (33) and inserts it at index 0, shifting everything else right, giving [33, 11, 99, 33] (4.8.A.5). The other choices either forget the set call's effect, place the inserted 33 at the wrong position, or evaluate box.get(2) using the list's state before set was called."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "A programmer wants the ArrayList codes, which starts empty as an ArrayList<String>, to contain [\"A1\", \"B2\", \"C3\"] after execution. Which of the following code segments produces this result?", "choices": ["codes.add(\"B2\");\ncodes.add(\"A1\");\ncodes.add(\"C3\");", "codes.add(\"A1\");\ncodes.add(\"C3\");\ncodes.add(1, \"B2\");", "codes.add(\"A1\");\ncodes.add(\"B2\");\ncodes.add(0, \"C3\");", "codes.add(\"C3\");\ncodes.add(\"B2\");\ncodes.add(0, \"A1\");"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Building [\"A1\", \"C3\"] and then calling add(1, \"B2\") inserts \"B2\" at index 1, shifting \"C3\" right and producing exactly [\"A1\", \"B2\", \"C3\"] (4.8.A.5). The first choice adds the names in the wrong order entirely; the third choice ends by inserting \"C3\" at index 0, producing [\"C3\", \"A1\", \"B2\"]; and the fourth choice ends by inserting \"A1\" at index 0, producing [\"A1\", \"C3\", \"B2\"], neither of which matches the target."},
  {"id": "4.8-intermediate-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.8.A.6", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> data = new ArrayList<Integer>();\ndata.add(4);\ndata.add(8);\ndata.add(12);\ndata.add(16);\nint sum = data.get(0) + data.get(data.size() - 1);\nWhat value is stored in sum after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["12", "16", "20", "24"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "After the four add calls, data is [4, 8, 12, 16]; data.get(0) returns 4, and data.size() - 1 evaluates to 3, so data.get(3) returns 16; sum is therefore 4 + 16, which is 20 (4.8.A.5, 4.8.A.6). The other choices result from using the wrong index for the last element, adding only one of the two values, or miscomputing size() - 1."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nnums.add(40);\nnums.add(15);\nnums.add(60);\nnums.add(15);\nnums.remove(2);\nWhat are the contents of nums after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[40, 15, 60, 15], since 2 does not appear in the list so nothing is removed", "[40, 60, 15]", "An IndexOutOfBoundsException is thrown, since there is no element equal to the value 2", "[40, 15, 15], since the int argument 2 is resolved to the index-based remove(int index) overload, removing the element at index 2"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Because nums is an ArrayList<Integer> and the literal 2 is a primitive int, the call nums.remove(2) resolves to the overload remove(int index), which removes the element AT INDEX 2 (the value 60), not the value 2, leaving [40, 15, 15] (4.8.A.5). The first and third choices incorrectly assume remove always searches for a matching value; the second choice removes an element but computes the wrong resulting list."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> log = new ArrayList<String>();\nlog.add(\"init\");\nlog.add(\"load\");\nlog.add(\"run\");\nlog.add(0, \"boot\");\nlog.set(2, \"exec\");\nlog.add(\"done\");\nlog.remove(1);\nlog.add(2, \"sync\");\nWhat are the contents of log after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[\"boot\", \"exec\", \"run\", \"sync\", \"done\"]", "[\"boot\", \"init\", \"exec\", \"sync\", \"run\", \"done\"]", "[\"boot\", \"exec\", \"sync\", \"run\", \"done\"]", "[\"boot\", \"sync\", \"exec\", \"run\", \"done\"]"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Tracing step by step: after the first three calls log is [\"init\", \"load\", \"run\"]; add(0, \"boot\") gives [\"boot\", \"init\", \"load\", \"run\"]; set(2, \"exec\") replaces index 2 (\"load\") with \"exec\", giving [\"boot\", \"init\", \"exec\", \"run\"]; add(\"done\") gives [\"boot\", \"init\", \"exec\", \"run\", \"done\"]; remove(1) removes \"init\", giving [\"boot\", \"exec\", \"run\", \"done\"]; add(2, \"sync\") inserts \"sync\" at index 2, shifting \"run\" and \"done\" right, giving [\"boot\", \"exec\", \"sync\", \"run\", \"done\"] (4.8.A.5). The other choices each drop or misplace one step of this sequence."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.3", "prompt": "Consider the following declarations.\nI. ArrayList<Integer> a = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nII. ArrayList<int> b = new ArrayList<int>();\nIII. ArrayList<Double> c = new ArrayList<>();\nIV. ArrayList d = new ArrayList<String>();\nV. ArrayList<String> e = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nWhich of the following identifies all the declarations that compile without error?", "choices": ["I, II, and III only", "I, III, and IV only", "I, III, IV, and V", "I and III only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Declaration I compiles because Integer is a valid reference type; III compiles because the diamond operator lets the compiler infer Double from the declared type; IV compiles because a raw ArrayList reference may refer to an ArrayList<String> object, though it triggers an unchecked-type warning (4.8.A.3, 4.8.A.4). Declaration II fails to compile because int is a primitive type and cannot be used as the generic type E, and declaration V fails to compile because ArrayList<String> and ArrayList<Integer> are different, incompatible generic types, so an ArrayList<Integer> object cannot be assigned to an ArrayList<String> variable."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.6", "prompt": "Consider the following method.\npublic static void shiftEnds(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    int last = list.size() - 1;\n    int firstVal = list.get(0);\n    list.set(0, list.get(last));\n    list.set(last, firstVal);\n    list.remove(last - 1);\n}\nWhich of the following best describes the precondition necessary for this method to execute without throwing an exception?", "choices": ["The list must contain at least 2 elements.", "The list must contain at least 1 element.", "The list must be sorted in ascending order.", "The list must contain only positive Integer values."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Both get(0) and set(0, ...) require the list to be non-empty, but the final call, remove(last - 1), requires last - 1 to be a valid index, meaning last - 1 must be at least 0, so last must be at least 1, which means size() must be at least 2 (4.8.A.6). A list with only 1 element would make last equal to 0 and last - 1 equal to -1, an invalid index that would throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException; the method's correctness does not depend on the list being sorted or on the sign of its values."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> scores = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nscores.add(90);\nscores.add(85);\nscores.add(100);\nInteger target = 85;\nscores.remove(target);\nscores.remove(1);\nWhat are the contents of scores after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[85, 100]", "[90, 85]", "[90, 100]", "[90]"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Because target is declared as an Integer object, scores.remove(target) resolves to the overload remove(Object o), which removes the first element equal in value to 85, leaving [90, 100]; the second call, scores.remove(1), uses a primitive int literal, so it resolves to remove(int index), removing the element AT INDEX 1 (the value 100), leaving [90] (4.8.A.5). The other choices confuse which overload is used for each call or apply the removals in the wrong order."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> vals = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nvals.add(2);\nvals.add(4);\nvals.add(6);\nvals.add(8);\nvals.add(2, 100);\nvals.remove(0);\nvals.set(2, 200);\nConsider the following statements about vals after this code segment executes.\nI. vals contains exactly 4 elements.\nII. vals.get(0) equals 100.\nIII. vals.get(2) equals 200.\nIV. vals contains the value 6.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, and III only", "I and III only", "I and III only", "II, III, and IV only"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Tracing the sequence: after the first four calls vals is [2, 4, 6, 8]; add(2, 100) gives [2, 4, 100, 6, 8]; remove(0) gives [4, 100, 6, 8]; set(2, 200) replaces index 2 (6) with 200, giving [4, 100, 200, 8] (4.8.A.5). This confirms statement I (the list has 4 elements) and statement III (index 2 is 200) are true, while statement II is false since get(0) is 4, not 100, and statement IV is false since the value 6 was overwritten by set and is no longer present."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.3", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> words = new ArrayList<String>();\nwords.add(\"cat\");\nwords.add(\"dog\");\nStringBuilder extra = new StringBuilder(\"fox\");\nwords.add(extra);\nWhy does this code segment fail to compile?", "choices": ["ArrayList objects cannot store more than 2 elements without being resized manually.", "words is declared as ArrayList<String>, so the compiler only allows String objects to be added; a StringBuilder object cannot be added even though both classes represent text.", "The add method can only be called once per ArrayList object.", "StringBuilder objects must be converted to int before being added to any ArrayList."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Declaring words as ArrayList<String> restricts add so that only String objects can be passed to it, and the compiler catches the attempt to add a StringBuilder object as a type error, which is exactly the benefit of using the generic ArrayList<E> over a raw ArrayList (4.8.A.3). ArrayLists resize automatically and place no cap on the number of elements; add may be called any number of times; and there is no requirement to convert objects to int before adding them to an ArrayList."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> seq = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nseq.add(5);\nseq.add(10);\nseq.add(15);\nseq.add(20);\nseq.set(1, seq.get(3));\nseq.add(0, seq.get(2));\nseq.remove(4);\nseq.add(seq.get(0));\nWhat are the contents of seq after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[15, 5, 20, 15, 15]", "[15, 5, 20, 15, 20]", "[5, 20, 15, 20, 15]", "[15, 5, 15, 20, 15]"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Tracing step by step: after the four add calls seq is [5, 10, 15, 20]; set(1, seq.get(3)) replaces index 1 (10) with 20, giving [5, 20, 15, 20]; add(0, seq.get(2)) inserts the current value at index 2 (15) at index 0, giving [15, 5, 20, 15, 20]; remove(4) removes index 4 (the trailing 20), giving [15, 5, 20, 15]; add(seq.get(0)) appends the current value at index 0 (15) to the end, giving [15, 5, 20, 15, 15] (4.8.A.5). The other choices each evaluate one of the get calls using a stale version of the list."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> pool = new ArrayList<Integer>();\npool.add(8);\npool.add(3);\npool.add(8);\npool.add(1);\npool.remove(1);\npool.remove(Integer.valueOf(1));\nConsider the following statements about pool after this code segment executes.\nI. The first pool.remove(1) call removes the element at index 1, since 1 is passed as a primitive int literal.\nII. The second pool.remove(Integer.valueOf(1)) call removes the first element equal to the value 1, since an Integer object is passed as the argument.\nIII. After both calls, pool contains [8, 8].\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I only", "I and II only", "II and III only", "I, II, and III"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The list begins as [8, 3, 8, 1]; the first call, pool.remove(1), passes a primitive int literal and resolves to remove(int index), removing the element at index 1 (the value 3), giving [8, 8, 1], confirming statement I; the second call, pool.remove(Integer.valueOf(1)), passes an Integer object and resolves to remove(Object o), removing the first element equal to the value 1, giving [8, 8], confirming statements II and III (4.8.A.5). All three statements accurately describe this sequence, so the complete and correct answer must include all three."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "A programmer wants the ArrayList grid, which starts empty as an ArrayList<Integer>, to contain [9, 3, 9, 6] after execution. Consider the following candidate code segments.\nI.\ngrid.add(9);\ngrid.add(3);\ngrid.add(9);\ngrid.add(6);\nII.\ngrid.add(9);\ngrid.add(6);\ngrid.add(1, 3);\ngrid.add(2, 9);\nIII.\ngrid.add(3);\ngrid.add(9);\ngrid.add(6);\ngrid.add(0, 9);\nIV.\ngrid.add(9);\ngrid.add(9);\ngrid.add(1, 6);\ngrid.add(3, 3);\nWhich of the following identifies all the candidates that result in grid containing [9, 3, 9, 6]?", "choices": ["II and III only", "I and IV only", "I, II, and III only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Candidate I appends the values in order, directly producing [9, 3, 9, 6]. Candidate II builds [9, 6], inserts 3 at index 1 to get [9, 3, 6], then inserts 9 at index 2 to get [9, 3, 9, 6]. Candidate III builds [3, 9, 6], then inserts 9 at index 0 to get [9, 3, 9, 6]. All three match the target list (4.8.A.5). Candidate IV builds [9, 9], inserts 6 at index 1 to get [9, 6, 9], then inserts 3 at index 3 (the end) to get [9, 6, 9, 3], which does not match the target."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.3", "prompt": "A student writes the following code segment, which does not compile.\nArrayList<double> readings = new ArrayList<double>();\nreadings.add(3.5);\nreadings.add(7.2);\nWhich of the following changes would fix the compile error while preserving the intended behavior?", "choices": ["Change add(3.5) to add(\"3.5\") and add(7.2) to add(\"7.2\").", "Change ArrayList<double> to ArrayList<Double> in the declaration and constructor call.", "Remove the generic type entirely, declaring the variable as ArrayList readings = new ArrayList();", "Change the constructor call to new ArrayList<double>(2);"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The generic type E in an ArrayList<E> declaration must be a reference type, and double is a primitive type, so replacing it with the wrapper class Double, as in ArrayList<Double>, fixes the compile error while keeping the list storing decimal values as intended (4.8.A.3). Converting the values to Strings would change the stored data type entirely rather than fixing the underlying issue; removing the generic type would compile but would give up the compiler's ability to catch type errors; and changing only the constructor argument does not address that double still cannot appear as a generic type."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.6", "prompt": "Consider the following method.\npublic static void rotate(ArrayList<Integer> data)\n{\n    int n = data.size();\n    int last = data.get(n - 1);\n    data.remove(n - 1);\n    data.add(0, last);\n    data.remove(2);\n}\nWhich of the following best describes the precondition necessary for this method to execute without throwing an exception?", "choices": ["The list must contain at least 3 elements.", "The list must contain at least 1 element.", "The list must contain at least 2 elements.", "The list must contain at least 4 elements."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The call data.get(n - 1) requires the list to be non-empty, but after removing the last element and reinserting it at the front, the list's size returns to n, so the final call, data.remove(2), requires index 2 to be valid, meaning n must be at least 3 (4.8.A.6). If the list had only 1 or 2 elements, the final remove(2) call would reference an index beyond the list's bounds and throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException, so at least 3 elements are required."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "A student expects the call nums.remove(4) to remove the value 4 from the ArrayList<Integer> nums, which currently contains [7, 4, 4, 9, 2]. After calling nums.remove(4), the student is confused to find that nums contains [7, 4, 4, 9]. Which best explains this result?", "choices": ["This is a bug in Java; remove(int) should always search for a matching value first.", "The list must have been modified by another method before this call executed.", "nums.remove(4) removed the first occurrence of the value 4, but a second 4 remained in the list, causing the confusion.", "Because the argument 4 is a primitive int literal, Java resolves the call to the index-based remove(int index) overload, which removed the element at index 4 (the value 2), not the value 4."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Since 4 is passed as a primitive int literal rather than an Integer object, the call resolves to the overload remove(int index), which removes the element AT INDEX 4, the value 2, leaving [7, 4, 4, 9] (4.8.A.5). This is expected, documented behavior of the two remove overloads, not a bug; no other method call is implied by the scenario; and the confusion is not about a repeated value 4, since neither 4 was removed at all."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> chain = new ArrayList<String>();\nchain.add(\"P\");\nchain.add(\"Q\");\nchain.add(\"R\");\nchain.add(1, \"S\");\nchain.set(3, \"T\");\nchain.remove(0);\nchain.add(1, chain.get(0));\nConsider the following statements about chain after this code segment executes.\nI. chain contains exactly 4 elements.\nII. chain.get(0) equals \"S\".\nIII. chain.get(2) equals \"S\".\nIV. chain contains the String \"T\".\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, and III only", "II, III, and IV only", "I, II, and IV only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Tracing the sequence: after the first three calls chain is [\"P\", \"Q\", \"R\"]; add(1, \"S\") gives [\"P\", \"S\", \"Q\", \"R\"]; set(3, \"T\") replaces index 3 (\"R\") with \"T\", giving [\"P\", \"S\", \"Q\", \"T\"]; remove(0) removes \"P\", giving [\"S\", \"Q\", \"T\"]; add(1, chain.get(0)) reads the current value at index 0 (\"S\") and inserts it at index 1, giving [\"S\", \"S\", \"Q\", \"T\"] (4.8.A.5). This confirms statement I (size 4), statement II (get(0) is \"S\"), and statement IV (\"T\" is present) are true, while statement III is false since get(2) is \"Q\", not \"S\"."},
  {"id": "4.8-complex-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.8", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.8.A.5", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> vals = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nvals.add(14);\nvals.add(6);\nvals.add(30);\nvals.add(9);\nif (vals.get(0) < vals.get(vals.size() - 1))\n{\n    int temp = vals.get(0);\n    vals.set(0, vals.get(vals.size() - 1));\n    vals.set(vals.size() - 1, temp);\n}\nWhich of the following best describes the behavior of this code segment?", "choices": ["It swaps the first and last elements whenever the first element is larger than the last; here 14 is larger than 9, so the values swap, leaving vals as [9, 6, 30, 14].", "It swaps the first and last elements only when the first element is smaller than the last; since 14 is not smaller than 9, no swap occurs and vals remains [14, 6, 30, 9].", "It swaps the first and last elements whenever they are equal; since 14 does not equal 9, no swap occurs and vals remains [14, 6, 30, 9].", "It always swaps the first and last elements exactly once, regardless of their values, leaving vals as [9, 6, 30, 14]."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The condition vals.get(0) < vals.get(vals.size() - 1) compares the first element (14) to the last element (9); since 14 is not smaller than 9, the condition is false, the if-block never executes, and vals remains [14, 6, 30, 9] (4.8.A.5, 4.8.A.6). The first and fourth choices incorrectly conclude that a swap occurs, misreading either the direction of the comparison or the fact that the swap is conditional, and the third choice misdescribes the condition as testing for equality rather than a less-than comparison."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.1", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> scores = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nscores.add(10);\nscores.add(20);\nscores.add(30);\nscores.add(40);\nint total = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < scores.size(); i++)\n{\n    total += scores.get(i);\n}\nSystem.out.println(total);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing the code segment?", "choices": ["100", "40", "4", "0"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The indexed for loop traverses every element from index 0 through size() - 1, adding each value to total, so total accumulates 10 + 20 + 30 + 40 = 100 (4.9.A.1). 40 reflects only the last element examined, 4 confuses the sum with the list's size(), and 0 incorrectly assumes total is never updated during the traversal."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.1", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> fruits = new ArrayList<String>();\nfruits.add(\"apple\");\nfruits.add(\"kiwi\");\nfruits.add(\"mango\");\nfruits.add(\"fig\");\nint count = 0;\nfor (String fruit : fruits)\n{\n    if (fruit.length() >= 4)\n    {\n        count++;\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(count);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing the code segment?", "choices": ["4", "3", "1", "2"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The enhanced for loop reads every String in fruits exactly once; \"apple\" (5), \"kiwi\" (4), and \"mango\" (5) all have length at least 4, while \"fig\" (3) does not, so count ends at 3 (4.9.A.1). 4 incorrectly counts \"fig\" as well, and 1 and 2 undercount by failing to include every qualifying fruit found during the traversal."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.3", "prompt": "An ArrayList named roster contains exactly 6 elements. Which of the following calls is guaranteed to throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException?", "choices": ["roster.get(0)", "roster.get(5)", "roster.get(6)", "roster.get(roster.size() - 1)"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The valid indices of a 6-element ArrayList run from 0 through size() - 1, which is 5, so roster.get(6) always falls outside that range and throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException (4.9.A.3). roster.get(0) and roster.get(5) are the first and last valid indices, and roster.get(roster.size() - 1) is simply another way of writing the valid last index, so none of those three throw an exception."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.1", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nnums.add(3);\nnums.add(8);\nnums.add(10);\nnums.add(5);\nnums.add(6);\nint evenCount = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (nums.get(i) % 2 == 0)\n    {\n        evenCount++;\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(evenCount);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing the code segment?", "choices": ["2", "4", "5", "3"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The indexed for loop examines every element in nums, and 8, 10, and 6 satisfy nums.get(i) % 2 == 0, giving evenCount a final value of 3 (4.9.A.1). 2 undercounts by missing one even value, 4 overcounts by including an odd value, and 5 mistakes the count for the total number of elements traversed."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.4", "prompt": "A programmer adds a new element to an ArrayList<String> inside the body of an enhanced for loop that is currently traversing that same list. What happens when this code executes?", "choices": ["The program throws a ConcurrentModificationException at run time.", "The program compiles and runs, silently skipping the newly added element.", "The program throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException at run time.", "The new element is simply ignored, and the loop completes normally."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Changing the size of an ArrayList while it is being traversed by an enhanced for loop causes the loop to throw a ConcurrentModificationException as soon as it detects the structural change (4.9.A.4). The loop does not simply skip the new element or ignore the call, and the failure is not caused by accessing an invalid index, so it is not an IndexOutOfBoundsException."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.1", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Double> temps = new ArrayList<Double>();\ntemps.add(98.6);\ntemps.add(99.1);\ntemps.add(97.4);\ndouble sum = 0.0;\nfor (double t : temps)\n{\n    sum += t;\n}\nSystem.out.println(sum);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing the code segment?", "choices": ["296.1", "295.1", "99.1", "3"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The enhanced for loop adds every element of temps to sum, and 98.6 + 99.1 + 97.4 equals 295.1 (4.9.A.1). 296.1 reflects an arithmetic slip, 99.1 is only the largest single value rather than the total, and 3 mistakes the count of elements traversed for the sum of their values."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.3", "prompt": "An ArrayList named data has size() equal to n, where n is greater than 0. Which expression always represents a valid index of the last element of data?", "choices": ["n", "n + 1", "n - 1", "0"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The valid indices of any ArrayList run from 0 through size() - 1, so n - 1 is always the index of the last element (4.9.A.3). n itself is one index past the end of the list and is never valid, n + 1 is even further out of range, and 0 is only the last element's index in the special case where n equals 1."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.1", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> words = new ArrayList<String>();\nwords.add(\"cat\");\nwords.add(\"dog\");\nwords.add(\"emu\");\nString result = \"\";\nfor (int i = 0; i < words.size(); i++)\n{\n    result += words.get(i) + \" \";\n}\nSystem.out.println(result);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing the code segment?", "choices": ["\"emu dog cat \"", "\"catdogemu\"", "\"cat dog emu\"", "\"cat dog emu \""], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The indexed for loop traverses words from index 0 to size() - 1 in order, appending each word followed by a space, so the final printed String is \"cat dog emu \" including the trailing space after the last word (4.9.A.1). Printing the words in reverse misreads the loop's direction, omitting the spaces ignores what the concatenation actually builds, and dropping the final trailing space forgets that a space is appended after every element, including the last."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.1", "prompt": "ArrayList<String> colors contains exactly 5 elements. Consider the following code segment.\nfor (int i = 0; i < colors.size(); i++)\n{\n    System.out.println(colors.get(i));\n}\nHow many times does the loop body execute?", "choices": ["5", "4", "6", "0"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "An indexed for loop with this header executes once for every valid index from 0 through size() - 1, so for a 5-element list the body runs exactly 5 times (4.9.A.1). 4 undercounts by one index, 6 would require accessing an invalid index beyond the list, and 0 would mean the loop never runs, which does not happen since colors is not empty."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.3", "prompt": "Which of the following loop headers correctly traverses every valid index of an ArrayList named vals, from the first element to the last, without ever accessing an invalid index?", "choices": ["for (int i = 0; i <= vals.size(); i++)", "for (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)", "for (int i = 0; i < vals.size() - 1; i++)", "for (int i = 1; i < vals.size(); i++)"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Since valid indices run from 0 through size() - 1, starting i at 0 and continuing while i < vals.size() visits every element exactly once without ever going out of bounds (4.9.A.1, 4.9.A.3). Using i <= vals.size() allows one final out-of-range access that throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException, i < vals.size() - 1 stops one element too early and skips the last element, and starting at i = 1 skips the first element entirely."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.1", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> teams = new ArrayList<String>();\nteams.add(\"Hawks\");\nteams.add(\"Owls\");\nteams.add(\"Hawks\");\nteams.add(\"Wolves\");\nint count = 0;\nfor (String team : teams)\n{\n    if (team.equals(\"Hawks\"))\n    {\n        count++;\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(count);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing the code segment?", "choices": ["1", "4", "2", "0"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The enhanced for loop compares every String in teams to \"Hawks\" using equals, and \"Hawks\" appears at two positions, so count ends at 2 (4.9.A.1). 1 undercounts by missing a match, 4 confuses the count with the total number of elements, and 0 incorrectly assumes no matches exist at all."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.4", "prompt": "A programmer wants to remove certain elements from an ArrayList while traversing it. Which type of loop should be avoided for this purpose, since removing an element from the list it is iterating over can cause a ConcurrentModificationException?", "choices": ["An indexed for loop that counts upward", "An indexed for loop that counts downward", "A while loop that manually tracks an index variable", "An enhanced for loop"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "An enhanced for loop relies on the list not being structurally changed during traversal, so removing an element inside one throws a ConcurrentModificationException (4.9.A.4). Indexed for loops that count upward or downward, and while loops that manually track an index, can all be written to safely remove elements as long as the index is adjusted carefully, so they do not need to be avoided in the same way."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.1", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<String>();\nlist.add(\"red\");\nlist.add(\"green\");\nlist.add(\"blue\");\nfor (int i = list.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--)\n{\n    System.out.println(list.get(i));\n}\nIn what order are the elements printed?", "choices": ["blue, green, red", "red, green, blue", "green, blue, red", "blue, red, green"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Starting i at list.size() - 1 and decrementing down to 0 visits the elements in reverse order, so the loop prints blue, then green, then red (4.9.A.1). Printing red, green, blue describes the original forward order rather than this backward traversal, and the remaining two choices scramble the elements into orders the loop does not actually produce."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.3", "prompt": "ArrayList<Integer> nums is known to contain at least one element. Which of the following calls is guaranteed to throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException, regardless of how many elements nums actually contains?", "choices": ["nums.get(0)", "nums.get(-1)", "nums.get(nums.size() - 1)", "nums.get(nums.size() / 2)"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A negative index is always outside the valid range of 0 through size() - 1 no matter what nums contains, so nums.get(-1) is guaranteed to throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException (4.9.A.3). nums.get(0) and nums.get(nums.size() - 1) are always valid for a non-empty list, and nums.get(nums.size() / 2) always lands on a valid middle index for a non-empty list as well."},
  {"id": "4.9-basic-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.9.A.1", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> vals = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nvals.add(5);\nvals.add(3);\nvals.add(5);\nvals.add(5);\nvals.add(2);\nint count = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) == 5)\n    {\n        count++;\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(count);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing the code segment?", "choices": ["2", "4", "3", "5"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The indexed for loop compares every element of vals to 5, and the value 5 appears at three positions, so count ends at 3 (4.9.A.1). 2 undercounts by missing a match, 4 overcounts by including a non-matching element, and 5 confuses the count with one of the list's own stored values."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> vals = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nvals.add(4);\nvals.add(-1);\nvals.add(-1);\nvals.add(7);\nvals.add(-2);\nvals.add(9);\nfor (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) < 0)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n        i--;\n    }\n}\nWhat are the contents of vals after the code segment executes?", "choices": ["[4, -1, 7, 9]", "[4, -1, -1, 7, 9]", "[4, 7, -2, 9]", "[4, 7, 9]"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Because i-- executes only inside the if block, the loop rechecks the same index immediately after every removal, so both adjacent negative values and the later negative value are all correctly removed, leaving [4, 7, 9] (4.9.A.2). The other choices assume the trace stops early or that an adjacent negative value gets skipped, which does not happen once the decrement is placed correctly inside the if statement."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> vals = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nvals.add(6);\nvals.add(-3);\nvals.add(8);\nvals.add(-5);\nvals.add(2);\nfor (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) < 0)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n    }\n}\nWhat are the contents of vals after the code segment executes?", "choices": ["[6, 8, 2]", "[6, 8, -5, 2]", "[6, -3, 8, 2]", "[6, 8]"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Because the two negative values are not adjacent in the original list, removing -3 does not cause -5 to be skipped, since -5 has not yet shifted into an already-checked index, so the loop still finds and removes it, correctly producing [6, 8, 2] (4.9.A.2). The other choices assume a value gets skipped or that an extra value was removed, neither of which happens for this particular list."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> vals = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nvals.add(7);\nvals.add(-2);\nvals.add(-4);\nvals.add(9);\nfor (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) < 0)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n    }\n}\nWhat are the contents of vals after the code segment executes?", "choices": ["[7, 9]", "[7, -4, 9]", "[7, -2, 9]", "[-4, 7, 9]"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "After -2 is removed from index 1, the value -4 shifts left into index 1, but the loop's i++ advances to index 2 before that position is ever checked again, so -4 is skipped and remains in the final list [7, -4, 9] (4.9.A.2). The other choices either assume both negative values were removed or misplace which negative value actually survives."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> vals = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nvals.add(7);\nvals.add(-2);\nvals.add(-4);\nvals.add(9);\nfor (int i = vals.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) < 0)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n    }\n}\nWhat are the contents of vals after the code segment executes?", "choices": ["[7, -4, 9]", "[7, -2, 9]", "[7, 9]", "[9, 7]"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Traversing backward means any shift caused by a removal only affects indices that have already been visited, so both -2 and -4 are correctly removed even though they are adjacent, leaving [7, 9] (4.9.A.2). The other choices reflect the forward-traversal skipping bug or an incorrect final ordering, neither of which applies when traversing backward."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.3", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> data = new ArrayList<Integer>();\ndata.add(1);\ndata.add(2);\ndata.add(3);\ndata.add(4);\nfor (int i = 1; i <= data.size(); i++)\n{\n    System.out.println(data.get(i));\n}\nWhat happens when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["It prints 1, 2, 3, and 4 with no error.", "It throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException immediately, before printing anything.", "It prints 2, 3, 4, and 1, then throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException.", "It prints 2, 3, and 4, then throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Starting i at 1 skips index 0 (the value 1), and the condition i <= data.size() allows i to reach 4, one past the last valid index of 3, so the loop prints 2, 3, and 4 before data.get(4) throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException (4.9.A.3). The loop never prints every original element since it never accesses index 0, and the exception does not occur until the fourth iteration rather than immediately."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "A programmer writes three candidate loop bodies, each intended to remove every element equal to 0 from an ArrayList<Integer> named vals.\nI.\nfor (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) == 0)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n        i--;\n    }\n}\nII.\nfor (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) == 0)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n    }\n}\nIII.\nfor (int i = vals.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) == 0)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n    }\n}\nWhich of these loop bodies correctly removes every 0 from vals, regardless of whether two 0 values are adjacent in the list?", "choices": ["I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II, and III", "II only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Rechecking the same index after a removal (I) and traversing backward (III) both correctly handle adjacent zeros, because neither lets the index advance past a value that has shifted into the just-checked position; only II, which always increments regardless of whether a removal occurred, can skip an adjacent zero (4.9.A.2). Any choice that includes II among the always-correct options, or excludes I or III, misjudges which strategies actually protect against the skip."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.4", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> tags = new ArrayList<String>();\ntags.add(\"red\");\ntags.add(\"blue\");\ntags.add(\"green\");\nfor (String tag : tags)\n{\n    if (tag.equals(\"blue\"))\n    {\n        tags.remove(tag);\n    }\n}\nWhat happens when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["It removes \"blue\" and prints the remaining tags with no error.", "It throws a ConcurrentModificationException at run time.", "It throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException at run time.", "It compiles but produces no output at all."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Removing an element from tags while an enhanced for loop is traversing it structurally modifies the list mid-iteration, which the enhanced for loop detects and reports by throwing a ConcurrentModificationException (4.9.A.4). No exception-free removal actually occurs, the failure is not caused by an invalid index access, and the code does attempt to execute rather than silently producing nothing."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.3", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment, where names is a properly initialized, non-empty ArrayList<String>.\nfor (int index = names.size(); index >= 0; index--)\n{\n    if (names.get(index).equals(target))\n    {\n        System.out.println(\"found\");\n    }\n}\nWhich of the following best describes what happens when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["It correctly traverses every element from last to first.", "It skips the last element but otherwise traverses correctly.", "It throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException on the very first access, since index starts at names.size(), one past the last valid index.", "It throws a ConcurrentModificationException on the first access."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The valid indices of names run from 0 to size() - 1, so starting index at names.size() means the very first call to names.get(index) is already out of range, throwing an IndexOutOfBoundsException before any element is examined (4.9.A.3). The loop cannot traverse correctly or even skip just the last element since it fails immediately, and this is an indexing error rather than a structural-modification error, so it is not a ConcurrentModificationException."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<String>();\nlist.add(\"a\");\nlist.add(\"x\");\nlist.add(\"x\");\nlist.add(\"b\");\nfor (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (list.get(i).equals(\"x\"))\n    {\n        list.remove(i);\n    }\n}\nWhat are the contents of list after the code segment executes?", "choices": ["[\"a\", \"b\"]", "[\"a\", \"x\", \"x\", \"b\"]", "[\"x\", \"a\", \"b\"]", "[\"a\", \"x\", \"b\"]"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "After the first \"x\" is removed from index 1, the second \"x\" shifts into index 1, but the loop's i++ moves on to index 2 before rechecking that position, so the second \"x\" is skipped and the final list is [\"a\", \"x\", \"b\"] (4.9.A.2). The other choices assume both \"x\" values were removed, that neither was removed, or scramble the surviving order."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider two loop bodies, each intended to remove every value less than 10 from the same ArrayList<Integer> containing [12, 8, 19, 3].\nLoop A:\nfor (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) < 10)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n        i--;\n    }\n}\nLoop B:\nfor (int i = vals.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) < 10)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n    }\n}\nDo Loop A and Loop B produce the same final contents when each is run separately on this list?", "choices": ["Yes, both produce [12, 19].", "No, Loop A produces [12, 19], but Loop B produces [12, 8, 19].", "No, Loop A produces [12, 8, 19, 3], but Loop B produces [12, 19].", "Yes, both produce [8, 3]."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Whether traversed forward with the index decremented after each removal or traversed backward, every value less than 10 (8 and 3) is correctly removed since both strategies protect against skipping an element after a shift, leaving [12, 19] in either case (4.9.A.2). The other choices either invent a difference between two equally correct strategies or list the removed values instead of the surviving ones."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.4", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> counts = new ArrayList<Integer>();\ncounts.add(5);\ncounts.add(10);\ncounts.add(15);\nfor (int c : counts)\n{\n    counts.add(c * 2);\n}\nWhat happens when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["It runs forever, since new elements keep getting added.", "It throws a ConcurrentModificationException at run time.", "It throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException at run time.", "It successfully doubles the size of counts with no error."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Adding an element to counts while an enhanced for loop is traversing it structurally modifies the list during iteration, which triggers a ConcurrentModificationException as soon as the loop attempts its next access (4.9.A.4). The loop does not run indefinitely because it fails with an exception almost immediately, the failure is not related to accessing an invalid index, and the addition is never allowed to complete successfully."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<String>();\nlist.add(\"no\");\nlist.add(\"yes\");\nlist.add(\"no\");\nlist.add(\"no\");\nlist.add(\"ok\");\nfor (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (list.get(i).equals(\"no\"))\n    {\n        list.remove(i);\n        i--;\n    }\n}\nWhat are the contents of list after the code segment executes?", "choices": ["[\"yes\", \"no\", \"ok\"]", "[\"no\", \"yes\", \"ok\"]", "[\"yes\", \"ok\"]", "[\"yes\", \"no\", \"no\", \"ok\"]"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Because i-- runs inside the if block every time a removal happens, the loop rechecks each shifted-in element immediately, correctly removing every \"no\", including the two adjacent ones, leaving [\"yes\", \"ok\"] (4.9.A.2). The other choices assume at least one \"no\" survives, which does not happen once the decrement is placed correctly."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "A programmer writes the following loop, intended to remove every element equal to 0 from an ArrayList<Integer> named vals.\nfor (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) == 0)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n    }\n}\nFor which of the following initial contents of vals does the loop fail to remove all the 0 values?", "choices": ["[1, 0, 2, 0]", "[5, 0, 6]", "[0, 3, 0]", "[0, 0, 3]"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Only [0, 0, 3] has two 0 values in strictly adjacent positions; after the first is removed the second shifts into the just-checked index, but i++ advances past it, leaving a 0 in the final list (4.9.A.2). The other three lists have no adjacent 0 values, so the index shift never causes an element to be skipped, and each is fully cleaned of zeros."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.3", "prompt": "A programmer writes the following loop condition to traverse an ArrayList<Integer> named vals.\nfor (int i = 0; i <= vals.size() - 1; i++)\nWhich statement best evaluates this loop condition?", "choices": ["It is equivalent to i < vals.size() and correctly visits every valid index.", "It always throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException on the last iteration.", "It skips the last element of vals.", "It skips the first element of vals."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Since i <= vals.size() - 1 is mathematically the same condition as i < vals.size(), this loop correctly visits every valid index from 0 through size() - 1 with no out-of-bounds access (4.9.A.3). It does not throw an exception, and it skips neither the last element nor the first, since i still starts at 0 and continues through the final valid index."},
  {"id": "4.9-intermediate-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.9.A.4", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> scores = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nscores.add(88);\nscores.add(91);\nscores.add(76);\nint total = 0;\nfor (int s : scores)\n{\n    total += s;\n}\nIs this loop at risk of throwing a ConcurrentModificationException?", "choices": ["Yes, because any enhanced for loop over an ArrayList risks this exception.", "No, because the loop body only reads values from scores and never adds or removes elements.", "Yes, because total is being modified during the loop.", "No, but only because scores contains fewer than 5 elements."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A ConcurrentModificationException occurs only when the list being traversed is structurally modified, meaning elements are added or removed, during iteration; this loop only reads values from scores to accumulate total, so it is completely safe (4.9.A.4). Not every enhanced for loop is at risk, only ones that modify the underlying list; modifying total, a separate variable, has no bearing on scores; and the list's size is irrelevant to whether the loop is safe."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following method.\npublic static void purge(ArrayList<Integer> vals, int target)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (vals.get(i) == target)\n        {\n            vals.remove(i);\n        }\n    }\n}\nThe method is intended to remove every occurrence of target from vals. Consider calling purge(vals, 7) separately on each of the following initial contents of vals.\nI. [7, 7, 2, 9]\nII. [2, 7, 9, 7]\nIII. [7, 2, 7, 9]\nIV. [2, 9, 4, 1]\nFor which of these does the call to purge fail to remove every 7?", "choices": ["I and III only", "II and IV only", "I only", "I, II, and III only"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Only list I, [7, 7, 2, 9], has two 7s in strictly adjacent positions; removing the first shifts the second into the just-checked index, and i++ then advances past it, leaving a 7 behind. In lists II and III the two 7s are separated by another value, so the second 7's new position after a shift lines up exactly with where the loop checks next, and it is still caught; list IV contains no 7 at all, so nothing goes wrong (4.9.A.2). Any choice that omits I, or includes II, III, or IV as failing, misjudges exactly which adjacency pattern causes the skip."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following method.\npublic static void purgeFixed(ArrayList<Integer> vals, int target)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (vals.get(i) == target)\n        {\n            vals.remove(i);\n            i--;\n        }\n    }\n}\nConsider calling purgeFixed(vals, 7) separately on each of the following initial contents of vals.\nI. [7, 7, 2, 9]\nII. [2, 7, 9, 7]\nIII. [7, 2, 7, 9]\nIV. [2, 9, 4, 1]\nFor which of these does the call to purgeFixed fail to remove every 7?", "choices": ["I only", "I and III only", "II and IV only", "None of these; purgeFixed correctly removes every 7 in each case."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Because i-- executes immediately after every removal, the loop always rechecks the index that a shifted element moved into before advancing, so no adjacency pattern, however many 7s are consecutive, can cause an element to be skipped in any of the four lists (4.9.A.2). Any choice naming a list as failing incorrectly assumes the decrement-based fix still has the same vulnerability as the unfixed version."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "A programmer considers five candidate strategies, each intended to remove every element equal to 0 from an ArrayList<Integer> named vals.\nI. Traverse forward from index 0, and after each removal, decrement the loop variable inside the if block before continuing.\nII. Traverse forward from index 0, and immediately break out of the loop after the first removal.\nIII. Traverse backward from index vals.size() - 1 down to 0, removing whenever a match is found.\nIV. Traverse forward from index 0, incrementing the loop variable unconditionally on every iteration regardless of whether a removal occurred.\nV. Traverse forward starting at index 1 instead of index 0, decrementing the loop variable inside the if block after each removal.\nWhich of these strategies correctly remove every 0 from vals, regardless of how many 0 values are adjacent?", "choices": ["I and III only", "I, III, and V only", "II and IV only", "I, II, III, IV, and V"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Rechecking the current index after a removal (I) and traversing backward (III) both correctly avoid ever skipping a shifted-in element, regardless of adjacency. II is wrong because breaking out of the loop after the first removal abandons the traversal early, IV is the classic bug that skips an element shifted into an already-passed index, and V is wrong because starting at index 1 permanently ignores whatever is at index 0 (4.9.A.2). Any choice that includes II, IV, or V, or excludes I or III, misidentifies which strategies are actually robust."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.4", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<String> logs = new ArrayList<String>();\nlogs.add(\"info\");\nlogs.add(\"error\");\nlogs.add(\"info\");\nlogs.add(\"warning\");\nfor (String entry : logs)\n{\n    if (entry.equals(\"info\"))\n    {\n        logs.remove(entry);\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(logs);\nWhat happens when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["It prints [\"error\", \"warning\"] with no error.", "It throws a ConcurrentModificationException the first time an \"info\" entry is removed.", "It prints [\"info\", \"error\", \"warning\"], since only one \"info\" is removed.", "It throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException once the loop reaches the end of logs."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Removing an element from logs while an enhanced for loop is actively traversing it structurally modifies the list mid-iteration; Java detects this and throws a ConcurrentModificationException as soon as the loop's next step occurs after the first removal (4.9.A.4). No output is ever printed because the exception halts execution before reaching the println statement, and the failure is a structural-modification error, not an out-of-bounds index access."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.4", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> queue = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nqueue.add(1);\nqueue.add(2);\nqueue.add(3);\nfor (int value : queue)\n{\n    if (value == 2)\n    {\n        queue.add(0, 99);\n    }\n}\nWhat happens when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["It inserts 99 at the front of queue and finishes the loop with no error.", "It throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException at run time.", "It throws a ConcurrentModificationException at run time.", "It creates an infinite loop that never terminates."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Inserting an element into queue while an enhanced for loop is traversing it structurally modifies the list during iteration, which triggers a ConcurrentModificationException as soon as the loop attempts to continue (4.9.A.4). The insertion is never allowed to finish successfully, the failure is unrelated to accessing an invalid index directly, and the loop does not run forever, since the exception halts it almost immediately."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.3", "prompt": "Consider the following method.\npublic static void clearMatches(int target, ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    for (int index = list.size(); index >= 0; index--) // Line 1\n    {\n        if (list.get(index) == target) // Line 2\n        {\n            list.remove(index);\n        }\n    }\n}\nThe method is intended to remove every value equal to target from its ArrayList parameter, traversing backward. However, it is not working as intended. Which of the following changes can be made so that the method works as intended?", "choices": ["In line 1, changing the loop condition to index > 0", "In line 2, changing the list access expression to list.get(target)", "In line 1, changing the loop increment/decrement to index++", "In line 1, changing the loop initialization to index = list.size() - 1"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The loop currently starts at index = list.size(), which is always one past the last valid index, so the very first call to list.get(index) throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException; starting instead at list.size() - 1 begins at the last valid index and lets the backward traversal work correctly (4.9.A.3). Changing the condition to index > 0 would still start out of bounds and would also skip index 0, changing list.get(target) checks an entirely unrelated value, and changing index-- to index++ would turn this into a forward traversal that still starts out of bounds."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following method.\npublic static void trim(ArrayList<Integer> list, int limit)\n{\n    int i = 0;\n    while (i < list.size())\n    {\n        if (list.get(i) > limit)\n        {\n            list.remove(i);\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            i++;\n        }\n    }\n}\nConsider the following statements about this method.\nI. The method correctly removes every element greater than limit, regardless of how many such elements are adjacent to each other.\nII. If two elements greater than limit are adjacent, the second one is skipped and remains in the list.\nIII. Calling this method on an empty ArrayList throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException.\nIV. The variable i is incremented only when the current element is not removed, which is what allows the same index to be rechecked after a removal.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I and IV only", "II and III only", "I, II, and IV only", "I, III, and IV only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Because i++ only executes in the else branch, the same index is automatically rechecked immediately after any removal, so every qualifying element is removed correctly even when several appear consecutively, making statements I and IV true (4.9.A.2). Statement II is false precisely because this method avoids that skip by only incrementing when nothing was removed, and statement III is false because calling trim on an empty list makes the while condition false immediately, so the loop body never executes and no exception occurs."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following method.\npublic static void purgeZeros(ArrayList<Integer> vals)\n{\n    int j = 0;\n    while (j < vals.size())\n    {\n        if (vals.get(j) == 0)\n        {\n            vals.remove(j);\n        }\n        j++;\n    }\n}\nThe method is intended to remove every 0 from vals. Consider calling purgeZeros separately on each of the following initial contents of vals.\nI. [0, 5, 0, 6]\nII. [5, 0, 0, 6]\nIII. [5, 6, 0]\nIV. [0, 0, 0, 6]\nFor which of these does purgeZeros fail to remove every 0?", "choices": ["I and III only", "II and IV only", "II only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Because j is incremented unconditionally every iteration regardless of whether a removal occurred, any list with two or more 0 values in strictly adjacent positions can cause a 0 to be skipped once it shifts into an already-passed index. This happens in list II, where the second adjacent 0 shifts into position 1 as j advances to 2, and in list IV, where the list shrinks below j's value before the last shifted 0 is ever rechecked. Lists I and III have no strictly adjacent 0 values, so every 0 is still found and removed correctly (4.9.A.2)."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following method.\npublic void purgeQuest(ArrayList<Integer> nums)\n{\n    int k = 0;\n    while (k < nums.size())\n    {\n        if (nums.get(k) == 9)\n        {\n            nums.remove(k);\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            k++;\n        }\n    }\n}\nAssume that nums initially contains [9, 9, 4, 1, 9, 2, 5]. What are the contents of nums after purgeQuest executes?", "choices": ["[9, 4, 1, 9, 2, 5]", "[4, 1, 9, 2, 5]", "[4, 1, 2, 5]", "[4, 9, 1, 2, 5]"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Because k is only incremented in the else branch, the same index is always rechecked immediately after a removal, so every 9 is correctly removed, including the two adjacent 9s at the start, leaving [4, 1, 2, 5] (4.9.A.2). The other choices reflect assuming some 9s survive the traversal or scrambling the order of the remaining elements."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.3", "prompt": "Assume list is a non-empty ArrayList<Integer>. A programmer considers three loop headers intended to traverse list, where each loop body only calls list.get(i) with no other statements.\nI. for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\nII. for (int i = 1; i <= list.size(); i++)\nIII. for (int i = list.size(); i > 0; i--)\nWhich of these loop headers is guaranteed to throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException on its very first access, regardless of what list contains?", "choices": ["I only", "II only", "I and III only", "III only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Loop III always starts at index list.size(), which is one past the last valid index for any non-empty list, so list.get(i) is guaranteed to fail on the very first access no matter what the list contains (4.9.A.3). Loop I starts within valid range and never goes out of bounds, and loop II only fails when list has exactly one element, since starting at index 1 is invalid only when size() equals 1; for any list with two or more elements, loop II's first access is valid, so it is not guaranteed to fail regardless of content."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following method.\npublic static void clean(ArrayList<Integer> vals)\n{\n    int i = 0;\n    while (i < vals.size())\n    {\n        int current = vals.get(i);\n        if (current == 1 || current == 2)\n        {\n            vals.remove(i);\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            i++;\n        }\n    }\n}\nAssume vals initially contains [1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 2, 4]. What are the contents of vals after clean executes?", "choices": ["[3, 4]", "[3, 2, 4]", "[1, 3, 4]", "[3, 1, 4]"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Because i is only incremented in the else branch, every occurrence of 1 or 2 is removed correctly regardless of how many appear consecutively, since the same index is always rechecked immediately after a removal, leaving only the non-matching values [3, 4] (4.9.A.2). The other choices assume at least one 1 or 2 survives the traversal, which does not happen with this increment-only-on-non-removal pattern."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.4", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment.\npublic static void addBonus(ArrayList<Integer> data)\n{\n    data.add(0);\n}\n// elsewhere in the same class:\nArrayList<Integer> points = new ArrayList<Integer>();\npoints.add(10);\npoints.add(20);\npoints.add(30);\nfor (int p : points)\n{\n    if (p == 20)\n    {\n        addBonus(points);\n    }\n}\nWhat happens when this code segment executes?", "choices": ["It runs with no error, since the modification happens inside a separate method rather than directly inside the loop body.", "It throws a ConcurrentModificationException, because points is structurally modified through addBonus while the enhanced for loop is traversing it.", "It throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException, because addBonus adds an element past the end of points.", "It silently ignores the call to addBonus and finishes the loop normally."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The enhanced for loop is traversing points, and calling addBonus, which adds an element to that same list object, still structurally modifies points even though the add call happens inside a different method; the loop still detects this modification and throws a ConcurrentModificationException (4.9.A.4). It does not matter that the modification is one method call removed from the loop body itself, the failure is not an out-of-bounds index access, and the call to addBonus is not silently ignored."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "Consider the following code segment, intended to remove every negative value from an ArrayList<Integer> named readings.\nfor (int i = 0; i < readings.size(); i++) // Line 1\n{\n    if (readings.get(i) < 0) // Line 2\n    {\n        readings.remove(i); // Line 3\n    }\n    i--; // Line 4\n}\nBecause line 4 executes on every iteration rather than only after a removal, this code does not work as intended. Consider calling it separately on each of the following initial contents of readings.\nI. [-3, 5, -2]\nII. [-1, -4, -7]\nIII. [4, 6, 8]\nIV. [5, -9, 2]\nFor which of these does this code segment fail to terminate (run forever) rather than completing with a correctly cleaned list?", "choices": ["II only", "I and III only", "I, III, and IV only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Because line 4's i-- executes every iteration rather than only after an actual removal, it cancels out the for loop's own i++, leaving i permanently stuck at 0 until either the list becomes empty or a non-negative value occupies index 0, at which point the loop spins forever without ever advancing. Lists I, III, and IV each contain at least one non-negative value, so the traversal becomes permanently stuck as soon as index 0 holds one, but list II consists entirely of negative values, so index 0 keeps getting removed until the list empties and the loop condition correctly becomes false, terminating with the correctly cleaned empty list (4.9.A.2)."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.3", "prompt": "Consider the following method.\npublic static void trimHigh(ArrayList<Integer> vals, int cutoff)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i <= vals.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (vals.get(i) > cutoff)\n        {\n            vals.remove(i);\n        }\n    }\n}\nConsider the following statements about this method.\nI. The loop condition i <= vals.size() allows i to reach an index that is always one past the last valid index of vals.\nII. If cutoff is greater than or equal to every element in vals, so that no elements are ever removed, this method will always throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException before completing.\nIII. Changing the loop condition to i < vals.size() alone would fully fix every bug in this method, including any elements skipped due to removal shifting the list.\nIV. Even if the condition were corrected to i < vals.size(), the method would still be vulnerable to skipping an element immediately following one that was removed.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I and II only", "II and IV only", "I, II, III, and IV", "I, II, and IV only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Statement I is true because the condition i <= vals.size() lets i take the value vals.size(), which is always one past the final valid index; statement II is true because when nothing is ever removed, the list's size never shrinks, so i eventually reaches that same out-of-range value and vals.get(i) throws; and statement IV is true because even after fixing the condition to i < vals.size(), the loop still lacks any mechanism to recheck a shifted-in index after a removal, so it remains vulnerable to skipping an element immediately following one that was removed (4.9.A.2, 4.9.A.3). Statement III is false because correcting the loop bound alone does not address the separate, independent bug of skipping elements after a removal-caused shift."},
  {"id": "4.9-complex-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.9", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.9.A.2", "prompt": "A programmer writes three implementations, each intended to remove every element equal to 8 from an ArrayList<Integer> named vals.\nI.\nfor (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) == 8)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n        i--;\n    }\n}\nII.\nfor (int i = 0; i < vals.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) == 8)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n    }\n}\nIII.\nfor (int i = vals.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--)\n{\n    if (vals.get(i) == 8)\n    {\n        vals.remove(i);\n    }\n}\nEach implementation is run separately on a fresh copy of the same list, [8, 8, 8, 3, 8, 5]. Which of these implementations produce the fully correct result of [3, 5]?", "choices": ["I and III only", "II and III only", "I only", "I, II, and III"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Rechecking the same index after a removal (I) and traversing backward (III) both correctly remove every 8, including the run of three consecutive 8s at the front, producing [3, 5] in each case; II leaves one 8 behind because after the first removal shifts the next 8 into the current index, i has already advanced past it, and this pattern of narrowly missed catches continues, so one 8 survives in the final list [8, 3, 5] (4.9.A.2). Any choice that includes II among the correct implementations, or excludes I or III, misjudges how the index-recheck and backward strategies actually behave on this list."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A weather station stores hourly temperature readings in an ArrayList<Integer> named temps. Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> temps = new ArrayList<Integer>();\ntemps.add(68);\ntemps.add(74);\ntemps.add(59);\ntemps.add(81);\ntemps.add(77);\nint maxTemp = temps.get(0);\nfor (int i = 1; i < temps.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (temps.get(i) > maxTemp)\n    {\n        maxTemp = temps.get(i);\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(maxTemp);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["81", "77", "68", "59"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The maximum-value algorithm initializes maxTemp to the first element and then updates it only when a strictly larger value is found while traversing the rest of the list, so maxTemp correctly ends at 81 (4.10.A.1, max algorithm). 77 is the second-largest value and would only be printed if the loop stopped one iteration early, 68 is simply the second element in the list, and 59 is the minimum value, which would result from an algorithm searching in the wrong direction."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A store tracks daily prices in an ArrayList<Double> named prices. A developer wants minPrice to end up storing the smallest value in prices.\ndouble minPrice = prices.get(0);\nfor (int i = 1; i < prices.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (/* missing condition */)\n    {\n        minPrice = prices.get(i);\n    }\n}\nWhich condition correctly replaces /* missing condition */ so that minPrice correctly stores the smallest value in prices?", "choices": ["prices.get(i) > minPrice", "prices.get(i) < minPrice", "prices.get(i) <= 0", "prices.get(i) == minPrice"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The minimum-value algorithm must replace the running minimum whenever a smaller element is found, so the condition prices.get(i) < minPrice is correct (4.10.A.1, min algorithm). Using > would instead find the maximum value, comparing to 0 checks against an unrelated fixed value rather than the running minimum, and using == would only update minPrice on an exact tie, which does not implement a minimum search at all."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A teacher stores quiz scores in an ArrayList<Integer> named scores. Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> scores = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nscores.add(15);\nscores.add(18);\nscores.add(12);\nscores.add(20);\nint total = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < scores.size(); i++)\n{\n    total += scores.get(i);\n}\nSystem.out.println(total);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["45", "18", "65", "20"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The sum algorithm initializes an accumulator to 0 and adds every element of scores to it exactly once, so total correctly ends at 15 + 18 + 12 + 20 = 65 (4.10.A.1, sum algorithm). 45 results from forgetting to add the final element, 18 and 20 are just single elements from the list rather than the running total of all four."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<Double> named distances has been initialized with the following values: [12.0, 8.0, 10.0, 6.0, 14.0]. Consider the following code segment.\ndouble total = 0.0;\nfor (double d : distances)\n{\n    total += d;\n}\nSystem.out.println(total / distances.size());\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["50.0", "8.0", "12.0", "10.0"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The average algorithm uses an enhanced for loop to accumulate the sum of every Double element (auto-unboxed into the double variable d) and then divides that sum by the number of elements, so the result is (12.0 + 8.0 + 10.0 + 6.0 + 14.0) / 5 = 10.0 (4.10.A.1, sum/average algorithm). 50.0 is the sum before it is divided by the size, and 8.0 and 12.0 are just individual elements mistaken for the computed average."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<Integer> named inventory holds the stock count of each item in a warehouse aisle. Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> inventory = new ArrayList<Integer>();\ninventory.add(5);\ninventory.add(0);\ninventory.add(3);\ninventory.add(8);\nboolean outOfStock = false;\nfor (int i = 0; i < inventory.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (inventory.get(i) == 0)\n    {\n        outOfStock = true;\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(outOfStock);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["false", "true", "0", "3"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "This is the exists-at-least-one algorithm: a boolean flag starts false and is set to true as soon as any element satisfies the property, and it is never reset back to false, so once inventory.get(1) equals 0, outOfStock stays true for the rest of the traversal (4.10.A.1). false would only print if no element were ever 0, and 0 and 3 are element values from the list, not the boolean result the code actually prints."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<Integer> named ages stores the ages of every member registered for a youth club. Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> ages = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nages.add(14);\nages.add(16);\nages.add(15);\nages.add(13);\nboolean allTeens = true;\nfor (int i = 0; i < ages.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (ages.get(i) < 13 || ages.get(i) > 19)\n    {\n        allTeens = false;\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(allTeens);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["true", "false", "13", "16"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "This is the all-elements algorithm: a boolean flag starts true and is only set to false if some element fails to satisfy the required property, so since every age in ages falls between 13 and 19, the condition never triggers and allTeens stays true (4.10.A.1). false would only print if some age fell outside the 13-19 range, and 13 and 16 are element values from the list rather than the boolean result printed."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.2", "prompt": "A teacher stores student names in an ArrayList<String> named names and their exam scores in a parallel ArrayList<Integer> named scores, where names.get(i) and scores.get(i) refer to the same student. Consider the following code segment, which is intended to count how many students passed with a score of at least 70.\nint passCount = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < names.size(); i++)\n{\n    if (scores.get(i) >= 70)\n    {\n        passCount += 1;\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(passCount);\nAssuming names is [\"Ana\", \"Ben\", \"Cy\", \"Deb\"] and scores is [82, 65, 70, 59], what is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["4", "3", "2", "1"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "This is the count-with-property algorithm applied across two ArrayList objects traversed simultaneously by a shared index i, counting an element from names as qualifying whenever the corresponding score is at least 70; Ana (82) and Cy (70) both qualify, so passCount ends at 2 (4.10.A.2). 4 wrongly assumes every student passed, 3 comes from treating the boundary score of exactly 70 as failing rather than passing, and 1 undercounts by missing that a score of exactly 70 does satisfy >= 70."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<Integer> named readings stores hourly sensor readings. Consider the following code segment, which is intended to count how many times a reading is greater than the reading immediately before it.\nArrayList<Integer> readings = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nreadings.add(4);\nreadings.add(9);\nreadings.add(6);\nreadings.add(11);\nreadings.add(3);\nint increaseCount = 0;\nint prev = readings.get(0);\nfor (int i = 1; i < readings.size(); i++)\n{\n    int curr = readings.get(i);\n    if (curr > prev)\n    {\n        increaseCount += 1;\n    }\n    prev = curr;\n}\nSystem.out.println(increaseCount);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["3", "1", "4", "2"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "This is the consecutive-pairs algorithm: prev is updated to curr at the end of every iteration so that each comparison checks one element against the element immediately before it; the qualifying pairs are (4, 9) and (6, 11), so increaseCount ends at 2 (4.10.A.1). 3 and 4 overcount by treating non-increasing pairs as increases, and 1 undercounts by missing one of the two qualifying pairs."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<String> named tags stores hashtags entered by a user. Consider the following code segment, which is intended to determine whether any hashtag appears more than once.\nArrayList<String> tags = new ArrayList<String>();\ntags.add(\"sun\");\ntags.add(\"beach\");\ntags.add(\"waves\");\ntags.add(\"sun\");\nboolean hasDuplicate = false;\nfor (int i = 0; i < tags.size(); i++)\n{\n    for (int j = i + 1; j < tags.size(); j++)\n    {\n        if (tags.get(i).equals(tags.get(j)))\n        {\n            hasDuplicate = true;\n        }\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(hasDuplicate);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["false", "true", "sun", "0"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "This is the duplicate-detection algorithm: the inner loop starts at j = i + 1 so every pair of distinct positions is compared exactly once, and since \"sun\" appears at both index 0 and index 3, hasDuplicate is set to true (4.10.A.1). false would only print if no two elements matched anywhere in the list, and \"sun\" and 0 are not boolean values at all, so neither could be what this code segment actually prints."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<Integer> named queue has been initialized with the following values: [10, 20, 30, 40]. Consider the following method.\npublic static void mystery(ArrayList<Integer> list, int n)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n    {\n        int first = list.remove(0);\n        list.add(first);\n    }\n}\nWhat are the contents of queue after the call mystery(queue, 2) executes?", "choices": ["[20, 30, 40, 10]", "[40, 10, 20, 30]", "[30, 40, 10, 20]", "[10, 20, 30, 40]"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "This is the left-rotate-by-one algorithm applied twice: each iteration removes the first element and appends it to the end, so after one rotation queue is [20, 30, 40, 10], and after a second rotation it becomes [30, 40, 10, 20] (4.10.A.1). [20, 30, 40, 10] shows the result after only one rotation, [40, 10, 20, 30] is the result a right-rotation would instead produce, and [10, 20, 30, 40] incorrectly shows the list as unchanged."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.2", "prompt": "An ArrayList<Integer> named original has been initialized with the values [7, 14, 21, 28]. A second, empty ArrayList<Integer> named rotated is created to hold a left-rotated copy, so every element of original is shifted one position earlier and the first element wraps around to the end. Consider the following code segment.\nfor (int i = 1; i < original.size(); i++)\n{\n    rotated.add(original.get(i));\n}\nrotated.add(original.get(0));\nWhat are the contents of rotated after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[14, 21, 28, 7]", "[7, 14, 21, 28]", "[28, 7, 14, 21]", "[7, 28, 21, 14]"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "This left-rotation-by-one algorithm traverses original starting at index 1 and appends each remaining element to rotated, then appends the wrapped-around first element last, using two ArrayList objects simultaneously since original supplies the values while rotated accumulates the result (4.10.A.2). [7, 14, 21, 28] is simply the unrotated original, [28, 7, 14, 21] is what a right-rotation would instead produce, and [7, 28, 21, 14] is not a valid rotation of original at all."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<String> named playlist has been initialized with the values [\"Intro\", \"Verse\", \"Chorus\", \"Outro\"]. Consider the following code segment, which is intended to reverse the order of the elements in playlist.\nint left = 0;\nint right = playlist.size() - 1;\nwhile (left < right)\n{\n    String temp = playlist.get(left);\n    playlist.set(left, playlist.get(right));\n    playlist.set(right, temp);\n    left += 1;\n    right -= 1;\n}\nWhat are the contents of playlist after this code segment executes?", "choices": ["[Intro, Verse, Chorus, Outro]", "[Outro, Verse, Chorus, Intro]", "[Outro, Chorus, Intro, Verse]", "[Outro, Chorus, Verse, Intro]"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "This is the reversal algorithm: two indices start at opposite ends and move toward the center, swapping the elements they reference at each step via set, until left is no longer less than right, correctly producing [Outro, Chorus, Verse, Intro] (4.10.A.1). [Intro, Verse, Chorus, Outro] is simply the original unreversed order, [Outro, Verse, Chorus, Intro] only reflects the outer swap and omits the inner swap of Verse and Chorus, and [Outro, Chorus, Intro, Verse] miscomputes where the swapped elements end up."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<Integer> named ranks has been initialized with the following values, already stored in increasing order: [3, 9, 12, 20]. Consider the following method.\npublic static void insertSorted(ArrayList<Integer> list, int value)\n{\n    int i = 0;\n    while (i < list.size() && list.get(i) < value)\n    {\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    list.add(i, value);\n}\nWhat are the contents of ranks after the call insertSorted(ranks, 10) executes?", "choices": ["[3, 9, 12, 10, 20]", "[3, 9, 10, 12, 20]", "[10, 3, 9, 12, 20]", "[3, 9, 12, 20, 10]"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "This is the sorted-insertion algorithm: the while loop advances i past every element smaller than value, stopping at the first index whose element is not smaller than 10, which is index 2 (holding 12), and then inserts 10 there, producing [3, 9, 10, 12, 20] (4.10.A.1). [3, 9, 12, 10, 20] inserts 10 one position too late, [10, 3, 9, 12, 20] incorrectly inserts at the very front regardless of value, and [3, 9, 12, 20, 10] simply appends 10 at the end without checking sorted order at all."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<String> named guestList currently contains [\"Amir\", \"Bella\", \"Cade\"]. A developer wants to insert \"Drew\" so that it becomes the second element in the list, producing [\"Amir\", \"Drew\", \"Bella\", \"Cade\"]. Which statement correctly accomplishes this?", "choices": ["guestList.add(1, \"Drew\");", "guestList.add(2, \"Drew\");", "guestList.set(1, \"Drew\");", "guestList.add(\"Drew\");"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The insertion algorithm uses add(int index, E obj), which inserts the given element at the specified index and shifts every later element back one position, so add(1, \"Drew\") correctly places \"Drew\" as the second element while preserving \"Bella\" and \"Cade\" (4.10.A.1, insert algorithm). add(2, \"Drew\") inserts one position too late, set(1, \"Drew\") overwrites \"Bella\" instead of inserting a new element, and the single-argument add appends \"Drew\" to the end of the list instead of inserting it."},
  {"id": "4.10-basic-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "basic", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<Integer> named cart stores item prices, and a store wants to remove every item priced at exactly $0 (free samples) before checkout. Consider the following code segment.\nArrayList<Integer> cart = new ArrayList<Integer>();\ncart.add(12);\ncart.add(0);\ncart.add(5);\ncart.add(0);\ncart.add(8);\nfor (int i = cart.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--)\n{\n    if (cart.get(i) == 0)\n    {\n        cart.remove(i);\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(cart);\nWhat is printed as a result of executing this code segment?", "choices": ["[12, 0, 5, 0, 8]", "[12, 5, 0, 8]", "[12, 5, 8]", "[5, 8]"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "This deletion algorithm traverses from the last index down to the first, so removing an element never disturbs the index of any element that has not yet been checked, correctly leaving cart as [12, 5, 8] (4.10.A.1, delete algorithm). [12, 0, 5, 0, 8] shows the original untouched list, [12, 5, 0, 8] leaves one qualifying element behind, and [5, 8] mistakenly removes the first non-zero element as well."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to return the largest value in an ArrayList<Integer>.\npublic static int findMax(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    int max = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (list.get(i) > max)\n        {\n            max = list.get(i);\n        }\n    }\n    return max;\n}\nFor which of the following ArrayList<Integer> arguments does findMax return an incorrect result?", "choices": ["[-5, -2, -9]", "[3, 7, 1]", "[10, 20, 5]", "[0, 4, 2]"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The method initializes max to 0 instead of to the first element of the list, so whenever every element is negative, as in [-5, -2, -9], no element is ever greater than 0 and the method incorrectly returns 0 instead of the true maximum, -2 (4.10.A.1, max algorithm). The other three lists each contain a value greater than 0, so the flawed initialization happens not to change the result for those particular arguments."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to return the average of the values in an ArrayList<Integer> list.\npublic static double average(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size() - 1; i++)\n    {\n        sum += list.get(i);\n    }\n    return sum / (double) list.size();\n}\nFor the call average(list) where list contains [4, 8, 12, 16], what value is returned, and why is this incorrect?", "choices": ["10.0 is returned; the method correctly computes the average of all four elements", "10.0 is returned; the loop should instead start at i = 1 to fix the error", "6.0 is returned; the loop's condition list.size() - 1 causes the last element to be skipped when accumulating sum, even though the method still divides by the full size", "6.0 is returned; the method incorrectly divides by list.size() - 1 instead of list.size()"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The loop condition i < list.size() - 1 stops one iteration early, so the sum only includes 4 + 8 + 12 = 24, but the method still divides by the full size() of 4, giving 24 / 4.0 = 6.0 instead of the correct average of 10.0 (4.10.A.1, sum/average algorithm). The method does not actually compute 10.0 at all, since the flaw is in the loop bound rather than in the division, so any answer claiming a correctly computed 10.0 or a division-based explanation misidentifies the bug."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "Two developers write methods intended to determine whether at least one account balance in an ArrayList<Integer> named balances is negative.\npublic static boolean hasNegative1(ArrayList<Integer> balances)\n{\n    boolean found = false;\n    for (int i = 0; i < balances.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (balances.get(i) < 0)\n        {\n            found = true;\n        }\n    }\n    return found;\n}\npublic static boolean hasNegative2(ArrayList<Integer> balances)\n{\n    boolean found = false;\n    for (int i = 0; i < balances.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (balances.get(i) < 0)\n        {\n            found = true;\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            found = false;\n        }\n    }\n    return found;\n}\nWhich statement correctly compares hasNegative1 and hasNegative2?", "choices": ["Neither method correctly determines whether at least one balance is negative", "Both methods are correct implementations of the exists-at-least-one algorithm", "hasNegative2 is correct, but hasNegative1 incorrectly leaves found set to true even after a later non-negative balance is seen", "hasNegative1 is correct, but hasNegative2 incorrectly resets found to false whenever a non-negative balance is seen, so it only reflects whether the last element is negative"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "hasNegative1 correctly implements the exists-at-least-one algorithm because found is only ever set to true and never reset, so it stays true once any negative balance is found (4.10.A.1). hasNegative2 incorrectly resets found back to false in its else branch whenever a non-negative balance is encountered, so its final result only reflects whether the very last balance checked is negative rather than whether any balance is negative, making it not a correct implementation, and hasNegative1 never leaves a stale true value since true is exactly what should persist once found."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to determine whether every score in an ArrayList<Integer> scores is at least 60 (passing).\npublic static boolean allPass(ArrayList<Integer> scores)\n{\n    boolean allPass = true;\n    for (int i = 0; i < scores.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (scores.get(i) >= 60)\n        {\n            allPass = false;\n        }\n    }\n    return allPass;\n}\nFor scores = [75, 82, 91], what does allPass(scores) return, and why is this incorrect?", "choices": ["true; the method correctly identifies that every score is passing", "false; the condition is inverted, setting allPass to false whenever a score does meet the threshold instead of when it fails to meet it", "false; the loop should use a while loop instead of a for loop", "true; but only because scores.size() is exactly 3"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The condition is backward: it sets allPass to false whenever a score DOES meet the passing threshold, rather than when a score fails to meet it, so for scores = [75, 82, 91], every iteration triggers the branch and the method incorrectly returns false even though every score is passing (4.10.A.1, all-elements algorithm). The method does not actually return true for this input, and switching to a while loop or changing the list's size would not fix the inverted condition, which is the real source of the error."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to count how many elements in an ArrayList<Integer> list are even.\npublic static int countEvens(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    int count = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size() - 1; i++)\n    {\n        if (list.get(i) % 2 == 0)\n        {\n            count += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}\nFor the call countEvens(list) where list contains [4, 7, 10, 9, 6], what value is returned, and why is this incorrect?", "choices": ["2; the loop condition list.size() - 1 causes the last element, 6, to never be checked", "3; the method correctly counts every even element", "2; the modulo operator is being used incorrectly for negative numbers", "3; but only because the list happens to have 5 elements"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The loop condition i < list.size() - 1 stops before the final index, so the last element, 6 (which is even), is never examined; only 4 and 10 among the checked elements are even, so the method returns 2 instead of the correct count of 3 (4.10.A.1, count-with-property algorithm). The method does not actually return 3, list contains no negative numbers to expose any modulo issue, and the flaw is the loop bound, not the size of the list itself."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "Two developers write methods intended to count how many pairs of adjacent elements in an ArrayList<Integer> readings are equal.\npublic static int countPairs1(ArrayList<Integer> readings)\n{\n    int count = 0;\n    int prev = readings.get(0);\n    for (int i = 1; i < readings.size(); i++)\n    {\n        int curr = readings.get(i);\n        if (curr == prev)\n        {\n            count += 1;\n        }\n        prev = curr;\n    }\n    return count;\n}\npublic static int countPairs2(ArrayList<Integer> readings)\n{\n    int count = 0;\n    int first = readings.get(0);\n    for (int i = 1; i < readings.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (readings.get(i) == first)\n        {\n            count += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}\nWhich statement correctly compares countPairs1 and countPairs2 for readings = [5, 5, 8, 8, 8]?", "choices": ["countPairs1 returns 1; countPairs2 returns 3, since countPairs2 is the correct adjacent-pair algorithm", "Both methods return 1, since neither accounts for repeated values correctly", "countPairs1 returns 3 and correctly counts adjacent equal pairs; countPairs2 returns 1 because it compares every element only to the first element instead of to its immediate predecessor", "Both methods return 3, since both correctly implement the adjacent-pair comparison algorithm"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "countPairs1 correctly implements the consecutive-pairs algorithm by updating prev to curr every iteration, so it correctly finds three adjacent equal pairs: (5, 5), (8, 8), and (8, 8), returning 3 (4.10.A.1). countPairs2 never updates its comparison value after initialization, so it compares every later element only to the fixed first element (5); only readings.get(1) equals 5, so it returns just 1, which does not correctly implement the consecutive-pairs algorithm even though it happens to also examine every element once."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to determine whether an ArrayList<String> list contains any duplicate values.\npublic static boolean hasDuplicates(ArrayList<String> list)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\n    {\n        for (int j = i; j < list.size(); j++)\n        {\n            if (list.get(i).equals(list.get(j)))\n            {\n                return true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\nWhat is the flaw in this implementation of the duplicate-detection algorithm?", "choices": ["The method should use == instead of equals to correctly compare String objects", "The inner loop starts at j = i instead of j = i + 1, so list.get(i) is always compared to itself and the method incorrectly returns true even when list has no duplicate values", "There is no flaw; the method correctly detects duplicate values", "The outer loop should start at i = 1 instead of i = 0"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Because the inner loop starts at j = i rather than j = i + 1, the very first comparison in every outer iteration compares list.get(i) to itself, which is always equal, so the method always returns true on its first comparison regardless of whether list actually contains a duplicate (4.10.A.1, duplicate-detection algorithm). Using equals rather than == is correct for comparing String contents, changing the outer loop's starting index would not address the self-comparison bug, and the method clearly does have this flaw rather than being correct."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.2", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to determine whether any name appears in both ArrayList<String> teamA and ArrayList<String> teamB.\npublic static boolean shareMember(ArrayList<String> teamA, ArrayList<String> teamB)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < teamA.size(); i++)\n    {\n        for (int j = 0; j < teamB.size(); j++)\n        {\n            if (teamA.get(i).equals(teamB.get(j)))\n            {\n                return true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\nWhich best evaluates this method as an implementation of the exists-at-least-one algorithm applied across two ArrayList objects simultaneously?", "choices": ["The method correctly returns true as soon as it finds any name that appears in both teamA and teamB, and returns false only if no such match is ever found after every pairing is checked", "The method is incorrect because it should use == instead of equals to compare String objects", "The method is incorrect because the outer loop should traverse teamB and the inner loop should traverse teamA", "The method is incorrect because it returns immediately on the first comparison, before every element has been checked"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "This method correctly implements exists-at-least-one across two ArrayList objects: it pairs every element of teamA with every element of teamB, returning true the moment a shared name is found and returning false only after every possible pairing has been examined without a match (4.10.A.2). Using equals rather than == is correct for comparing String contents, swapping which list is the outer versus inner loop would not change the correctness of the result, and returning early upon finding a match is exactly how an exists-at-least-one algorithm should behave, not a flaw."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to rotate every element of an ArrayList<Integer> list one position to the right, so the last element moves to the front.\npublic static void rotateRight(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    int last = list.remove(list.size() - 1);\n    list.add(last);\n}\nFor list = [3, 6, 9, 12], what does list contain after rotateRight(list) executes, and why is this incorrect?", "choices": ["[12, 3, 6, 9]; the method correctly rotates the list to the right", "[9, 12, 3, 6]; the method rotates the list left instead of right", "[3, 6, 9, 12]; the method correctly leaves the list unchanged since rotating by one has no visible effect", "[3, 6, 9, 12]; the list is unchanged because add(last) appends 12 to the end instead of inserting it at index 0"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The last element, 12, is correctly removed from the end, but list.add(last) appends it back to the end of the list rather than inserting it at index 0, so list ends up unchanged as [3, 6, 9, 12] instead of the correctly rotated [12, 3, 6, 9] (4.10.A.1, shift/rotate algorithm). The method does not actually rotate the list at all, rotating by one always changes a list with more than one distinct arrangement, and the method rotates in neither direction correctly because the removed value is never reinserted at the front."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "Two developers write methods intended to rotate every element of an ArrayList<Integer> list one position to the left, moving the first element to the end.\npublic static void rotateLeftA(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    int first = list.remove(0);\n    list.add(first);\n}\npublic static void rotateLeftB(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    int first = list.get(0);\n    list.add(first);\n}\nFor list = [2, 4, 6, 8], which statement correctly compares the results of rotateLeftA and rotateLeftB?", "choices": ["Both methods correctly produce [4, 6, 8, 2]", "rotateLeftA correctly produces [4, 6, 8, 2]; rotateLeftB incorrectly produces [2, 4, 6, 8, 2] because it duplicates the first element instead of removing it before re-adding it", "rotateLeftA incorrectly produces [2, 4, 6, 8, 2]; rotateLeftB correctly produces [4, 6, 8, 2]", "Both methods incorrectly leave the list as [2, 4, 6, 8]"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "rotateLeftA removes the first element with remove(0) before adding it back, correctly producing [4, 6, 8, 2] (4.10.A.1, shift/rotate algorithm). rotateLeftB uses get(0) instead of remove(0), so the first element is never taken out of the list before being appended again, producing [2, 4, 6, 8, 2], which both has the wrong contents and grows the list by one element instead of rotating it, so rotateLeftB does not correctly implement the rotation algorithm."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to reverse the order of the elements in an ArrayList<Integer> list in place.\npublic static void reverse(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\n    {\n        int temp = list.get(i);\n        list.set(i, list.get(list.size() - 1 - i));\n        list.set(list.size() - 1 - i, temp);\n    }\n}\nFor list = [1, 2, 3, 4], what does list contain after reverse(list) executes, and why is this incorrect?", "choices": ["[4, 3, 2, 1]; the method correctly reverses the list", "[4, 3, 2, 1]; but only because list.size() happens to be even", "[1, 2, 3, 4]; the loop continues past the midpoint and swaps every pair back to its original position, since it should stop once i reaches list.size() / 2", "[1, 4, 3, 2]; the method only swaps the first and last elements"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Tracing the loop shows that each pair of positions gets swapped once while i is small and swapped back again once i passes the midpoint and reaches the mirrored index, so after all four iterations the list ends up unchanged as [1, 2, 3, 4] instead of reversed (4.10.A.1, reversal algorithm); the loop should instead stop once i reaches list.size() / 2. The method does not actually produce [4, 3, 2, 1] for this or any list, since the double-swap problem is caused by the loop running for every index rather than by the list's size being even or odd, and more than just the first and last elements are affected by the flawed loop."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to insert value into an already-sorted ArrayList<Integer> list so that list remains sorted in increasing order.\npublic static void insertSorted(ArrayList<Integer> list, int value)\n{\n    int i = 0;\n    while (i < list.size() && list.get(i) > value)\n    {\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    list.add(i, value);\n}\nFor list = [2, 5, 9, 14] (sorted increasing), what does list contain after insertSorted(list, 7) executes, and why is this incorrect?", "choices": ["[2, 5, 7, 9, 14]; the method correctly inserts 7 in sorted position", "[2, 5, 9, 14, 7]; the method should use add(list.size(), value) instead", "[7, 2, 5, 9, 14]; the loop bound should be i <= list.size() instead of i < list.size()", "[7, 2, 5, 9, 14]; the comparison should be list.get(i) < value, not list.get(i) > value, so the loop stops immediately instead of scanning past the smaller elements"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Because the condition compares list.get(i) > value instead of list.get(i) < value, the loop stops on its very first check (since list.get(0) = 2 is not greater than 7), so i never advances past the smaller elements and 7 is inserted at index 0, producing [7, 2, 5, 9, 14] instead of the correctly sorted [2, 5, 7, 9, 14] (4.10.A.1, sorted-insertion algorithm). The method does not actually insert 7 in correct sorted position, and the flaw is the direction of the comparison rather than the loop's upper bound or the specific index used in the final add call."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "Two developers write methods that each build and return a new ArrayList<Integer> containing the integers from 0 to n - 1, in that order.\npublic static ArrayList<Integer> buildA(int n)\n{\n    ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<Integer>();\n    for (int k = 0; k < n; k++)\n    {\n        result.add(k);\n    }\n    return result;\n}\npublic static ArrayList<Integer> buildB(int n)\n{\n    ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<Integer>();\n    for (int k = n - 1; k >= 0; k--)\n    {\n        result.add(0, k);\n    }\n    return result;\n}\nWhich statement correctly compares buildA and buildB?", "choices": ["Both methods produce the same list contents, but buildA is faster than buildB because add(int index, E obj) must shift existing elements while add(E obj) does not", "Both methods produce the same list contents and run equally fast, since both call add() the same number of times", "The two methods produce different list contents, and buildA is faster than buildB", "The two methods produce different list contents, and buildB is faster than buildA"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Tracing buildB shows that inserting each value at index 0 while counting down from n - 1 to 0 produces the same increasing sequence [0, 1, ..., n - 1] as buildA, so both methods are correct and produce identical results; however, buildA's add(k) appends in O(1) amortized time while buildB's add(0, k) must shift every existing element forward each time, making buildA faster overall even though the two methods agree on every output (4.10.A.1, insert algorithm). The two methods do not produce different contents, and calling add() the same number of times does not mean each call costs the same amount of work."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "Two developers write methods intended to remove every String equal to \"skip\" from an ArrayList<String> list.\npublic static void removeA(ArrayList<String> list)\n{\n    ArrayList<String> kept = new ArrayList<String>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (!list.get(i).equals(\"skip\"))\n        {\n            kept.add(list.get(i));\n        }\n    }\n    list.clear();\n    for (int i = 0; i < kept.size(); i++)\n    {\n        list.add(kept.get(i));\n    }\n}\npublic static void removeB(ArrayList<String> list)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (list.get(i).equals(\"skip\"))\n        {\n            list.remove(i);\n        }\n    }\n}\nFor list = [\"go\", \"skip\", \"skip\", \"run\"], which statement correctly compares removeA and removeB as implementations of the delete-elements algorithm?", "choices": ["Both methods correctly produce [go, run]", "Both methods incorrectly produce [go, skip, skip, run], since ArrayList does not support removing elements while iterating", "removeA correctly produces [go, run]; removeB incorrectly produces [go, skip, run] because removing an element shifts the next element into the current index, which the loop then skips over", "removeA incorrectly produces [go, skip, run]; removeB correctly produces [go, run]"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "removeA correctly implements the delete algorithm by first building a separate kept list of every element that should survive, then copying that list back, so it always produces [go, run] (4.10.A.1). removeB removes elements directly from list while advancing i by one every iteration, so when the first \"skip\" at index 1 is removed, the second \"skip\" shifts into index 1 and is skipped over when i advances to 2, leaving one \"skip\" behind as [go, skip, run]; ArrayList does allow removal during a loop like this, it simply requires care about how indices shift, which removeB fails to account for."},
  {"id": "4.10-intermediate-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "intermediate", "lo": "4.10.A.2", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to determine whether ArrayList<Integer> listA and ArrayList<Integer> listB, which are known to be the same size, contain exactly the same values in the same order.\npublic static boolean sameContents(ArrayList<Integer> listA, ArrayList<Integer> listB)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < listA.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (listA.get(i).equals(listB.get(i)))\n        {\n            return true;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\nWhat is the flaw in this implementation of the all-elements-match algorithm applied across listA and listB simultaneously?", "choices": ["The method should compare listA.get(i) to listB.get(j) using two separate loop variables", "The method returns true as soon as it finds a single matching pair of elements, instead of confirming that every corresponding pair matches before returning true", "There is no flaw; the method correctly determines whether the two lists are identical", "The method incorrectly uses equals instead of == to compare Integer objects"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Because the method returns true the moment it finds any single matching pair at the same index, rather than requiring every corresponding pair across both lists to match, it will incorrectly report two very different lists as identical whenever they happen to share even one value in the same position (4.10.A.2). A correct all-elements-match algorithm across two lists traversed simultaneously must instead return false as soon as any mismatch is found and only return true after every index has been checked; comparing at the same shared index i (not two separate variables) is correct here, and using equals to compare Integer objects by value is the appropriate comparison, not the flaw."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-01", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to return the smallest value in a non-empty ArrayList<Integer> list.\npublic static int findMin(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    int min = list.get(0);\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (list.get(i) < min)\n        {\n            min = list.get(i);\n        }\n    }\n    return min;\n}\nConsider the following statements about findMin.\nI. For list = [4, 4, 4], findMin(list) returns 4.\nII. Comparing list.get(0) to min in the first loop iteration is redundant but does not cause an incorrect result.\nIII. If the loop instead started at i = 1, the method would still return the correct minimum for every possible non-empty list.\nIV. If min were instead initialized to 0 rather than list.get(0), the method would return an incorrect result for list = [5, 8, 3].\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I and IV only", "I, II, III, and IV", "I, II, and III only", "II and III only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "All four statements are true (4.10.A.1, min algorithm): I is true since every element is 4, so min correctly stays 4; II is true because comparing list.get(0) to itself at i = 0 is a harmless redundant comparison that never changes min; III is true because min already equals list.get(0) before the loop runs, so starting at i = 1 skips only that redundant first comparison without affecting correctness; and IV is true because initializing min to 0 would leave it at 0 for the all-positive list [5, 8, 3], since no element is ever less than 0, giving the incorrect result 0 instead of the true minimum, 3."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-02", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to return the average of the values in an ArrayList<Double> list, which is guaranteed to contain at least one element.\npublic static double average(ArrayList<Double> list)\n{\n    double sum = 0.0;\n    for (Double value : list)\n    {\n        sum += value;\n    }\n    return sum / list.size();\n}\nConsider the following statements about average.\nI. For list containing [10.0, 20.0, 30.0], average(list) returns 20.0.\nII. Because sum is declared as a double and value is autoboxed as a Double, the addition sum += value performs automatic unboxing without any compile-time error.\nIII. Dividing sum by list.size() uses integer division, so the result is always rounded down to the nearest whole number.\nIV. If list contained a single element, such as [7.5], average(list) would return 7.5.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, III, and IV only", "I and II only", "I, II, and IV only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "I is true since (10.0 + 20.0 + 30.0) / 3 = 20.0, II is true since Java automatically unboxes the Double value into a double when it is added to sum, and IV is true since a single-element list simply returns that element divided by 1 (4.10.A.1, sum/average algorithm). III is false because sum is declared as a double, so sum / list.size() is a double-by-int division that performs ordinary floating-point division rather than integer division, and the result is not truncated toward zero."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-03", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes two methods that each examine an ArrayList<Integer> temps of daily high temperatures.\npublic static boolean hasFreezing(ArrayList<Integer> temps)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < temps.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (temps.get(i) < 32)\n        {\n            return true;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\npublic static boolean allFreezing(ArrayList<Integer> temps)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < temps.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (temps.get(i) >= 32)\n        {\n            return false;\n        }\n    }\n    return true;\n}\nConsider the following statements about these two methods, applied to temps = [28, 35, 30, 40].\nI. hasFreezing(temps) returns true.\nII. allFreezing(temps) returns false.\nIII. Both methods use an early-return style rather than accumulating into a boolean flag through the entire loop.\nIV. If temps contained only [40, 45, 50], both hasFreezing(temps) and allFreezing(temps) would return true.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, and III only", "I, II, III, and IV", "II and III only", "I and IV only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "I is true because hasFreezing finds 28 < 32 on its very first check and returns true immediately (4.10.A.1, exists-at-least-one algorithm); II is true because allFreezing finds 35 >= 32 and returns false immediately (4.10.A.1, all-elements algorithm); and III correctly describes both methods, since each returns a value directly inside the loop rather than updating a boolean flag that is only returned after the loop completes. IV is false because for [40, 45, 50], no temperature is below 32, so hasFreezing returns false, and the very first temperature already fails the freezing requirement, so allFreezing also returns false, meaning both methods return false rather than true for that list."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-04", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "Three developers write methods intended to count how many Strings in an ArrayList<String> words have a length greater than 4.\npublic static int countI(ArrayList<String> words)\n{\n    int count = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < words.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (words.get(i).length() > 4)\n        {\n            count += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}\npublic static int countII(ArrayList<String> words)\n{\n    int count = 0;\n    for (String w : words)\n    {\n        if (w.length() >= 4)\n        {\n            count += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}\npublic static int countIII(ArrayList<String> words)\n{\n    int count = words.size();\n    for (int i = 0; i < words.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (words.get(i).length() <= 4)\n        {\n            count -= 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}\nFor words = [\"cat\", \"tiger\", \"lion\", \"panda\"], which method(s) correctly count how many words have a length greater than 4?", "choices": ["countI and countII only", "countII and countIII only", "countI, countII, and countIII", "countI and countIII only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "With lengths cat=3, tiger=5, lion=4, panda=5, exactly two words (tiger and panda) have length greater than 4. countI directly checks length() > 4 and correctly returns 2 (4.10.A.1, count-with-property algorithm). countIII starts count at the total size and subtracts once for every word with length() <= 4 (cat and lion), which is mathematically equivalent to counting words with length greater than 4, also correctly returning 2. countII uses length() >= 4 instead of > 4, so it incorrectly also counts lion (length exactly 4) as qualifying, returning 3, so countII is not a correct implementation for this required condition."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-05", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "An ArrayList<Integer> named prices tracks a stock's closing price each day: [50, 58, 55, 63, 60, 70]. Consider the following method.\npublic static int countBigJumps(ArrayList<Integer> prices)\n{\n    int jumps = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i < prices.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (prices.get(i) - prices.get(i - 1) > 5)\n        {\n            jumps += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    return jumps;\n}\nConsider the following statements about countBigJumps applied to prices = [50, 58, 55, 63, 60, 70].\nI. countBigJumps(prices) returns 3.\nII. The comparison prices.get(i) - prices.get(i - 1) > 5 examines each consecutive pair of elements exactly once.\nIII. If the loop instead started at i = 0, calling prices.get(-1) when i = 0 would throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException.\nIV. Reversing the order of elements in prices before calling countBigJumps would necessarily produce the same return value.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I and II only", "I, II, and III only", "I, III, and IV only", "I, II, III, and IV"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "I is true because the day-to-day differences are 8, -3, 8, -3, and 10, exactly three of which exceed 5, so jumps correctly ends at 3 (4.10.A.1, consecutive-pairs algorithm); II is true because starting the loop at i = 1 and comparing prices.get(i) to prices.get(i - 1) checks each of the five adjacent pairs exactly once. III is false because accessing an invalid index like prices.get(-1) throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException, not an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException (that exception type is specific to raw arrays, not ArrayList), so the statement misidentifies the exception type. IV is false because reversing the list also reverses the sign of every difference; tracing the reversed list [70, 60, 63, 55, 58, 50] shows none of its differences exceed 5, giving 0 big jumps instead of 3, so the return value is not necessarily preserved by reversal."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-06", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to determine whether an ArrayList<Integer> list contains any duplicate values.\npublic static boolean hasDuplicate(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\n    {\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < list.size(); j++)\n        {\n            if (list.get(i).equals(list.get(j)))\n            {\n                return true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\nConsider the following statements about hasDuplicate.\nI. For list = [3, 8, 3, 5], hasDuplicate(list) returns true.\nII. For list = [1, 2, 3, 4], hasDuplicate(list) returns false.\nIII. Starting the inner loop at j = i instead of j = i + 1 would cause the method to incorrectly return true for every non-empty list.\nIV. The method performs the same number of comparisons regardless of where in the list a duplicate occurs.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, III, and IV", "II and III only", "I, II, and III only", "I and IV only"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "I is true because 3 appears at both index 0 and index 2, so the method returns true (4.10.A.1, duplicate-detection algorithm); II is true because every value in [1, 2, 3, 4] is distinct, so no pair ever matches and the method returns false; and III is true because starting the inner loop at j = i would compare list.get(i) to itself on the very first check of every outer iteration, which is always true, causing hasDuplicate to incorrectly return true for any non-empty list. IV is false because the method returns immediately once a duplicate is found, so a list with a duplicate near the front requires far fewer comparisons than a list with no duplicates at all, which must check every possible pair before returning false."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-07", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "Two developers write methods intended to determine whether an ArrayList<String> tags contains any duplicate values.\npublic static boolean checkA(ArrayList<String> tags)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < tags.size(); i++)\n    {\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < tags.size(); j++)\n        {\n            if (tags.get(i).equals(tags.get(j)))\n            {\n                return true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\npublic static boolean checkB(ArrayList<String> tags)\n{\n    boolean found = false;\n    for (int i = 0; i < tags.size(); i++)\n    {\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < tags.size(); j++)\n        {\n            if (tags.get(i).equals(tags.get(j)))\n            {\n                found = true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return found;\n}\nWhich statement correctly compares checkA and checkB as implementations of the duplicate-detection algorithm?", "choices": ["Both methods always produce the same result, and checkA is never slower than checkB, since checkA can return early while checkB always finishes examining every pair", "Both methods always produce the same result and always take exactly the same amount of time, since both compare the same pairs of elements", "The two methods can produce different results, since checkB overwrites found back to false in some cases", "checkB is always faster than checkA, since it does not use a return statement inside the nested loop"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Both methods examine the same pairs under the same condition, so they always agree on whether a duplicate exists, meaning correctness is identical between them (4.10.A.1, duplicate-detection algorithm). However, checkA exits immediately via return true the moment it finds a matching pair, while checkB has no early exit and always finishes checking every remaining pair before returning found, so checkA can never take more time than checkB and is strictly faster whenever a duplicate is found before the very last pair is checked. found in checkB is only ever set to true and never reset back to false, so the two methods cannot disagree on their boolean result, and checkB's lack of an early return makes it no faster than checkA, not faster."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-08", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "Three developers each write a method intended to rotate every element of an ArrayList<Integer> list two positions to the right (so the last two elements move to the front, in order).\npublic static void rotateI(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    for (int k = 0; k < 2; k++)\n    {\n        int last = list.remove(list.size() - 1);\n        list.add(0, last);\n    }\n}\npublic static void rotateII(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    for (int k = 0; k < 2; k++)\n    {\n        int last = list.remove(list.size() - 1);\n        list.add(last);\n    }\n}\npublic static void rotateIII(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    int lastTwoStart = list.size() - 2;\n    int a = list.remove(lastTwoStart);\n    int b = list.remove(lastTwoStart);\n    list.add(0, a);\n    list.add(0, b);\n}\nFor list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], which method(s) correctly rotate the list two positions to the right, producing [4, 5, 1, 2, 3]?", "choices": ["rotateI and rotateIII only", "rotateII and rotateIII only", "rotateI, rotateII, and rotateIII", "rotateI only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Tracing rotateI: removing 5 and inserting it at index 0 gives [5, 1, 2, 3, 4], then removing 4 and inserting it at index 0 gives [4, 5, 1, 2, 3], which correctly matches the target (4.10.A.1, shift/rotate algorithm). rotateII uses add(last), which appends instead of inserting at the front, so both values are simply removed from the end and added right back, leaving the list unchanged as [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]. rotateIII removes 4 from index 3 (leaving [1, 2, 3, 5]) and then removes 5, which has shifted into index 3 (leaving [1, 2, 3]), and finally inserts them at the front in the order a then b, producing [5, 4, 1, 2, 3] \u2014 the last two elements end up swapped relative to their original order, so rotateIII does not produce the target result either. Only rotateI is correct."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-09", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "Two developers write methods intended to return a new ArrayList<Integer> containing the same elements as list but rotated one position to the left (the first element moved to the end), without modifying the original list.\npublic static ArrayList<Integer> rotateA(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    ArrayList<Integer> copy = new ArrayList<Integer>(list);\n    int first = copy.remove(0);\n    copy.add(first);\n    return copy;\n}\npublic static ArrayList<Integer> rotateB(ArrayList<Integer> list)\n{\n    ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<Integer>();\n    for (int i = 1; i < list.size(); i++)\n    {\n        result.add(list.get(i));\n    }\n    result.add(list.get(0));\n    return result;\n}\nWhich statement correctly compares rotateA and rotateB for a list with n elements?", "choices": ["Both methods produce the same resulting contents and are equally efficient, since both examine every element exactly once", "Both methods produce the same resulting contents; rotateA's call to remove(0) requires shifting every remaining element, while rotateB avoids any such shifting by only appending to the end", "The two methods produce different resulting contents, since rotateA modifies a copy while rotateB does not", "rotateB produces incorrect results because its loop never accesses index 0 directly inside the loop"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Both methods correctly produce the same left-rotated result: rotateA removes the first element and appends it back onto a copy of list, and rotateB copies every element starting from index 1 and then appends the original first element last, so both correctly implement the shift/rotate algorithm (4.10.A.1). The two methods are not equally efficient, though: rotateA's copy.remove(0) must shift every one of the remaining n - 1 elements down by one index as a single O(n) operation, while rotateB's loop only ever calls get and appends to the end with add, each of which is O(1) amortized, so rotateB avoids the index-shifting cost that rotateA incurs. rotateA modifying a copy rather than the original list does not change the resulting contents of that copy, and rotateB's use of list.get(0) after the loop is a correct and necessary part of the algorithm, not an error."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-10", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "Two developers write methods intended to reverse the order of the elements in an ArrayList<String> list.\npublic static void reverseA(ArrayList<String> list)\n{\n    int left = 0;\n    int right = list.size() - 1;\n    while (left < right)\n    {\n        String temp = list.get(left);\n        list.set(left, list.get(right));\n        list.set(right, temp);\n        left += 1;\n        right -= 1;\n    }\n}\npublic static void reverseB(ArrayList<String> list)\n{\n    ArrayList<String> result = new ArrayList<String>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\n    {\n        result.add(0, list.get(i));\n    }\n    list.clear();\n    for (int i = 0; i < result.size(); i++)\n    {\n        list.add(result.get(i));\n    }\n}\nWhich statement correctly compares reverseA and reverseB as implementations of the reversal algorithm?", "choices": ["Both methods correctly reverse the list and take the same amount of time, since both examine every element exactly once", "reverseA correctly reverses the list, but reverseB produces the elements in their original order instead of reversed", "Both methods correctly reverse the list, but reverseB is slower than reverseA because each call to add(0, ...) inside its loop must shift every element already in result, making reverseB's loop take quadratic time overall while reverseA's swaps take only linear time", "reverseB is faster than reverseA, since reverseA requires two separate index variables"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "reverseA correctly reverses list using roughly n / 2 swaps between mirrored positions, each an O(1) operation via get and set, giving an overall O(n) algorithm (4.10.A.1, reversal algorithm). reverseB is also correct: inserting each successive element at index 0 of result builds the elements up in reverse order, which is then copied back into list, so both methods produce the identical, correctly reversed contents. However, reverseB's loop calls result.add(0, list.get(i)) n times, and every one of those calls must shift all of the elements currently in result forward by one index, making reverseB's loop take quadratic time overall, unlike reverseA's linear-time swapping; reverseA's use of two index variables has no bearing on its speed relative to reverseB."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-11", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to insert value into an already-sorted ArrayList<Integer> list (sorted in increasing order) so that list remains sorted.\npublic static void insertSorted(ArrayList<Integer> list, int value)\n{\n    int i = 0;\n    while (i < list.size() && list.get(i) < value)\n    {\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    list.add(i, value);\n}\nConsider the following statements about insertSorted.\nI. For list = [2, 4, 6] and value = 4, calling insertSorted(list, 4) produces [2, 4, 4, 6].\nII. For list = [2, 4, 6] and value = 1, calling insertSorted(list, 1) produces [1, 2, 4, 6].\nIII. For list = [2, 4, 6] and value = 9, calling insertSorted(list, 9) produces [2, 4, 6, 9].\nIV. Because the loop condition uses strictly less than (<) rather than less than or equal to (<=), the new value is always inserted after every existing element equal to it, so it appears to the right of any duplicates already present.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, and III only", "I, II, III, and IV", "I and III only", "II and IV only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "I is true: i advances past the 2 (since 2 < 4) but stops at index 1 because 4 is not less than 4, so add(1, 4) produces [2, 4, 4, 6] (4.10.A.1, sorted-insertion algorithm). II is true: since 2 is not less than 1, i stays at 0 and add(0, 1) produces [1, 2, 4, 6]. III is true: every element is less than 9, so i advances all the way to list.size(), and add(3, 9) correctly appends 9 at the end, producing [2, 4, 6, 9]. IV is false, and statement I itself disproves it: when value equals an existing element (4), the loop stops at that element's index rather than past it, so the new 4 is inserted immediately before the original 4, placing it to the left of the existing duplicate, not to the right."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-12", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "Two developers write methods that each build and return a new ArrayList<Integer> containing the squares 0, 1, 4, 9, ..., (n - 1)^2 in that increasing order.\npublic static ArrayList<Integer> squaresA(int n)\n{\n    ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<Integer>();\n    for (int k = 0; k < n; k++)\n    {\n        result.add(k * k);\n    }\n    return result;\n}\npublic static ArrayList<Integer> squaresB(int n)\n{\n    ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<Integer>();\n    for (int k = n - 1; k >= 0; k--)\n    {\n        result.add(0, k * k);\n    }\n    return result;\n}\nWhich statement correctly compares squaresA and squaresB?", "choices": ["Both methods produce the same list contents and take the same amount of time, since each performs exactly n calls to add", "The two methods produce different list contents, and squaresA is faster than squaresB", "The two methods produce different list contents, and squaresB is faster than squaresA", "Both methods produce the same list contents, and squaresA is faster than squaresB because add(int index, E obj) must shift every existing element while add(E obj) does not"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Tracing squaresB for a small n confirms that inserting each square at index 0 while counting k down from n - 1 to 0 builds the same increasing sequence 0, 1, 4, ..., (n - 1)^2 as squaresA, so the two methods always agree on the resulting list contents (4.10.A.1, insert algorithm). They are not equally fast, though: squaresA's result.add(k * k) appends to the end in O(1) amortized time, while squaresB's result.add(0, k * k) must shift every element already in result forward by one index on every call, making squaresB's overall running time quadratic in n while squaresA's remains linear, even though both methods call add() exactly n times."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-13", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.1", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to remove every String from ArrayList<String> list whose length is less than 3.\npublic static void removeShort(ArrayList<String> list)\n{\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)\n    {\n        if (list.get(i).length() < 3)\n        {\n            list.remove(i);\n        }\n    }\n}\nConsider the following statements about removeShort.\nI. For list = [\"to\", \"cat\", \"an\", \"dog\"], removeShort(list) correctly leaves list as [\"cat\", \"dog\"].\nII. The flaw in this method is that removing an element shifts the next element into the current index, which the loop then skips by advancing i, so a short String immediately following another short String may survive.\nIII. Changing the loop to traverse from list.size() - 1 down to 0 would fix this flaw, since removing an element from the end backward never disturbs the indices of elements not yet visited.\nIV. This method is guaranteed to correctly remove every qualifying String as long as list contains no two consecutive Strings that both have length less than 3.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, and III only", "II, III, and IV only", "I, II, III, and IV", "I and IV only"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "I is true: tracing the loop on [\"to\", \"cat\", \"an\", \"dog\"] shows that \"to\" and \"an\" are the two qualifying Strings, and since they are not adjacent to each other in the original list, removing \"to\" at index 0 does not cause \"an\" to be skipped, so the method correctly produces [\"cat\", \"dog\"] (4.10.A.1, delete algorithm). II correctly describes the general flaw in this style of forward-traversal removal, and III correctly describes the standard fix, since traversing backward means every removal only affects indices that have already been processed. IV is true because the described flaw can only cause a qualifying element to be skipped when it immediately follows another element that was just removed, so a list with no two consecutive short Strings never triggers that failure mode, matching exactly what happened in the trace for statement I."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-14", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.2", "prompt": "A developer writes the following method, intended to merge two ArrayLists of Integers, listA and listB, each already sorted in increasing order, into a single new ArrayList that is also sorted in increasing order.\npublic static ArrayList<Integer> merge(ArrayList<Integer> listA, ArrayList<Integer> listB)\n{\n    ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<Integer>();\n    int i = 0;\n    int j = 0;\n    while (i < listA.size() && j < listB.size())\n    {\n        if (listA.get(i) <= listB.get(j))\n        {\n            result.add(listA.get(i));\n            i += 1;\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            result.add(listB.get(j));\n            j += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    while (i < listA.size())\n    {\n        result.add(listA.get(i));\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    while (j < listB.size())\n    {\n        result.add(listB.get(j));\n        j += 1;\n    }\n    return result;\n}\nConsider the following statements about merge, applied to listA = [2, 5, 9] and listB = [1, 5, 8, 12].\nI. merge(listA, listB) returns [1, 2, 5, 5, 8, 9, 12].\nII. The first while loop advances through listA and listB simultaneously, always appending whichever of the two current elements is smaller (or listA's element on a tie).\nIII. If the first while loop were removed entirely, leaving only the last two while loops, the method would still return a correctly sorted result for every possible pair of sorted input lists.\nIV. The two final while loops exist to append any remaining elements from whichever list still has elements left after the other has been fully traversed.\nWhich of the following identifies all the true statements?", "choices": ["I, II, III, and IV", "I, II, and IV only", "I and II only", "II, III, and IV only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Tracing the merge produces [1, 2, 5, 5, 8, 9, 12], confirming I, and the trace also confirms II: at each step the smaller of listA.get(i) and listB.get(j) is appended, with listA's element chosen on a tie (as happens when both are 5), advancing whichever index was just used (4.10.A.2, this is the standard simultaneous-traversal merge algorithm). IV correctly describes the purpose of the two trailing while loops, which drain whatever elements remain in the list that was not fully exhausted by the first loop. III is false because without the first loop, i and j never advance during it, so the two trailing loops would simply append all of listA in order followed by all of listB in order, producing [2, 5, 9, 1, 5, 8, 12] for this example, which is not sorted."},
  {"id": "4.10-complex-15", "course": "csa", "topic": "4.10", "tier": "complex", "lo": "4.10.A.2", "prompt": "An ArrayList<Integer> named source has been initialized with the values [40, 10, 90]. A second, empty ArrayList<Integer> named target is also created. After the code segment executes, target should contain [10, 90, 40] \u2014 every element of source shifted one position to the left, with the first element wrapped around to the end. Which of the following code segments correctly produces this result?", "choices": ["for (Integer temp : source)\n{\n    target.add(0, temp);\n}", "int n = source.size();\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n{\n    source.add(2, source.remove(0));\n    target.add(source.get(0));\n}", "for (Integer temp : source)\n{\n    target.add(temp);\n}", "for (int i = 1; i < source.size(); i++)\n{\n    target.add(source.get(i));\n}"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Tracing option B: removing 40 from index 0 and reinserting it at index 2 gives source = [10, 90, 40], and target.add(source.get(0)) appends 10; the next iteration similarly rotates source to [90, 40, 10] and appends 90 to target; the final iteration rotates source back to [40, 10, 90] and appends 40, leaving target = [10, 90, 40] as required (4.10.A.2, rotation using two ArrayList objects traversed simultaneously). Option A inserts each element of source at the front of target, which reverses the order and produces [90, 10, 40]; option C simply copies source into target in its original order, producing the unrotated [40, 10, 90]; and option D skips index 0 of source entirely and never wraps the first element around, producing the 2-element list [10, 90], which is missing 40 altogether."},
];
